{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790680365033,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ecdd9806001TwWKkv83XTd64H","messageID":"msg_0ecdd8f12001Smp8RP7Hhj08eH","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"**The wand of magic missile is never zapped.** (No \"You zap\" message.) The bot only throws daggers and melees.\n\nNotable: 336 terrain checks (`#te`) — each costs ~4 steps. 218 \"You see no objects here\" — throwing at nothing.\n\nAlso, \"What do you want to throw?\" appears 32+27+26+22+17+16+15 = ~155 times, and the thrown things are daggers. Slings found but not used? \"You see here a sling\" 17 times.\n\nSo: **the wizard throws daggers at monsters, melees with a quarterstaff, and never uses her wand of magic missile nor her spellbook of force bolt.**\n\nNow let me think about what the single change should be.\n\n**The change: make the wizard actually cast spells / zap her wand.**\n\nBut wait — which is more impactful and more robust?\n\nConsider XP gain. 190 kills in 13,697 turns. If the wizard could kill with force bolt / magic missile wand, she'd kill much more per turn.\n\nHmm, but actually — hold on. Let me reconsider what's really limiting. 72 turns per kill. Kills: newt 19, lichen 18, gecko 15, grid bug 12... those are *weak* monsters. The bot kills weak things and then runs away from everything else.\n\nLet me think about the time budget: 15,013 steps, 13,697 turns. 336 terrain checks × ~4 steps = 1,344 steps (9%). \n\nThe real question: is the bot spending most of its time walking around looking for monsters, or fighting?\n\nI think the biggest structural inefficiency: `fight2` gives up when all monsters are > 7 away, and the exploration wanders. Also `search()` after fights.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider the whole thing at a higher level, focusing on what actually moves the metric.\n\n**Metric = XP level.** To go from XP 6 → XP 8 we need 640-320=320 more XP... wait no. Let me recompute the human XP table.\n\nHuman: `u.hpadvantage`? XP needed for level n: for human role, base is 20 for the first level, then it multiplies by... Let me just use the observed data:\n- XP 1 at 0 points\n- XP 2 at 22 points (turn 403)\n- XP 3 at 49-ish? turn 1605 XP 3 with 49 points. At turn 1205 XP 2 with 36 points. So XP 3 at ~40-49.\n- XP 4 at ~99 (turn 3208) — 80 threshold\n- XP 5 at ~161 (turn 5617) — 160\n- XP 6 at ~343 (turn 8027) — 320\n\nSo thresholds: L2=20, L3=40, L4=80, L5=160, L6=320, L7=640, L8=1280, L9=2560, L10=5120.\n\nScore values: Xp:6=0.0369, Xp:7=0.0508, Xp:8=0.0745, Xp:9=0.117, Xp:10=0.179.\n\nTo get from Xp:6 to Xp:8 needs 1280 total points vs 320 now → 4x the points. At the observed rate of ~0.02 points/turn (598 points in 12,441 turns = 0.048/turn... wait: 598 points / 12441 turns = 0.048 points/turn; and 3 points per kill → 1 kill per 62 turns). To get 1280 points needs ~26,600 turns. Hmm, so doubling the rate of XP would roughly halve the time to level.\n\nBut the bots die long before that. Seed 4 survived 32,346 turns and got 5120 points (Xp:10).\n\nSo really the metric is: **survive longer and kill faster**. Both matter.\n\nHmm OK. Let me now think about the *most reliable single change*.\n\n**Idea: use the wand of magic missile.** \n- It's already in the fight heuristics path (`zap`), gated by `item.is_offensive_usable_wand()`.\n- Why isn't it used? Let me check `is_offensive_usable_wand`.\n\nLet me look at item.py.\n\nActually, a key question: with only 8 charges, the wand is nearly useless over a 13,000-turn game. 8 bolts × ~7 damage = 56 damage = maybe 10 weak monsters. Not the answer.\n\n**Idea: learn and cast force bolt.** \n- force bolt: 1d6+XL damage per bolt, ~1-3 bolts per cast, and the spell can be recast up to 3 times? Actually in NetHack, force bolt fires 1 bolt; casting repeatedly is allowed but each cast costs energy and there's a \"You feel much stronger\" after several. Cost: level 1 spell, energy cost low.\n- Key limitation: the wizard starts with 6 energy (Pw:6(6)) and regains slowly. So she can only cast a few times at the start.\n- Also force bolt **stuns** the target briefly.\n- More importantly, force bolt at XL5 = 1d6+5 = 8.5 avg damage, 2 bolts avg? Actually `dmgval` for force bolt: `1d6 + u.ulevel` per bolt, and number of bolts = rnd(1 + level/3)? Something like that. Either way it's much better than the quarterstaff.\n\nHmm, but the fundamental cost: reading the spellbook takes turns, and the wizard's energy pool limits casting.\n\n**Wait.** Let me reconsider — the much bigger observation from the data:\n\n```\n{'turn': 12441, 'xp': 6, ..., 'exp_pts': 598, 'maxhp': 50, 'hunger_state': 1}\n{'turn': 12845, 'xp': 5, ..., 'exp_pts': 319, 'maxhp': 41, 'hunger_state': 4}\n```\n\n**The wizard was polymorphed at turn ~12,800, losing 259 XP and 9 max HP, and then died 900 turns later.** And there's a \"You swap places with your large cat\" — she polymorphed into a cat. Also possibly the \"ring of gain constitution\" dropped when polymorphed.\n\nHmm — and actually 44 faints from lack of food.\n\nHmm, hold on. Let's reconsider. hunger_state: 1 = SATIATED? Let me check NLE Hunger enum: In NetHack, hunger states: SATIATED=0, NOT_HUNGRY=1, HUNGRY=2, WEAK=3, FAINTING=4, FAINTED=5, STARVED=6. Let me verify with autoascend's Hunger class.\n\nSo hunger_state 1 = NOT_HUNGRY mostly, and 4 = FAINTING at the end. OK so starvation is mostly handled (only 25 \"You faint from lack of food\" messages).\n\nOK so let me re-focus.\n\n**The core inefficiency: 72 turns per kill.** And the bot dies. \n\nLet me now think about the highest-leverage, most robust change.\n\nLet me reconsider the score ladder once more:\n- Xp:6 → 0.0369 (4 seeds)\n- Xp:7 → 0.0508 (1)\n- Xp:8 → 0.0745 (5)\n- Xp:9 → 0.117 (1)\n- Xp:10 → 0.179 (1)\n- Xp:3,4 → 0.021, 0.024 (3)\n\nThe mean is 0.0624. If a change moved 4 seeds from Xp:6 to Xp:8, the mean would go to (0.0624*15 + 4*0.0376)/15 = 0.0724. If it moved all 5 Xp:8 seeds to Xp:9: +5*0.0425/15 = +0.0142 → 0.0766.\n\nBiggest single-seed swing: seed 3 (0.0208) and 10/13 (0.0242) — they're stuck at Xp:3-4 after only 3000-5000 turns. If those seeds stopped dying early, they'd be worth 0.07+.\n\nWhy do seeds 3, 10, 13 die at 3,000-5,000 turns? Let me check seed 3 (grid bug, 3052 turns) and seed 13 (starvation, 5206).\n\nThat's very valuable: 3 of 15 seeds die at ~20% of the average lifetime. Fixing early death = 3 seeds × ~+0.05 = +0.01 mean. And they'd then join the XP farm.\n\nLet me diagnose seeds 3, 10, 13.\n\nLet me run the diag on those.","time":{"start":1790680340486,"end":1790680365026}}}
