{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790680650890,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ece207c9001q2vkMQRU4CgvFh","messageID":"msg_0ece1fe89001hPjh9Q6jR69ll8","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"The cast view works and the popup format matches `parse_spellcast_view`'s regex exactly:\n```\n'a - force bolt             1   attack         0%  91%-100%'\n```\nRegex: `^([a-zA-Z]) - *(force bolt|...) *([0-9]*) *(attack|...) *([0-9]*)\\% *([0-9]*\\%|\\(gone\\))` — matches. \n\nNow, the full pipeline is feasible:\n1. Read spellbook → learn spell.\n2. `parse_spellcast_view()` (just remove the HEALER-only guard).\n3. `agent.cast('force bolt', direction)` — already implemented.\n\nNow let me check the cast action: `Z` then `a` then direction. Let me test casting at a monster.\n\nAlso I need to check: does `agent.cast` work as written? Looking at:\n```python\ndef type_letters():\n    if 'You are too impaired' in self.message:\n        return\n    yield self.character.known_spells[spell_name]\n    for _ in range(3):\n        if 'In what direction?' in self.message:\n            break\n        yield ' '\n    if 'In what direction?' in self.message:\n        success[0] = True\n        yield direction\n\nself.step(A.Command.CAST, type_letters())\n```\nThe `self.step(action, additional_action_iterator)` mechanism: in `update()`, when an iterator is given, it pulls the next action, steps with it recursively. So: step CAST → update → next(iterator) yields the letter → step(letter) → update → ... The check `if 'In what direction?' in self.message` uses `self.message` which accumulates during the atom operation. Should work.\n\nBut careful: force bolt asks \"In what direction?\" — yes.\n\nNow: what about energy? `Pw:6(6)` and force bolt costs... Let's test whether the cast succeeds at 6 energy.\n\nLet me now think carefully about **the design of the change**, because this must be ONE focused change that is robust.\n\n### Proposed change: \"The wizard uses magic.\"\n\nConcretely:\n1. **Learn spells**: add a strategy to read a spellbook from the ground/inventory when the bot knows no spell or when a spellbook with a new attack spell appears. Simpler: at the start, read any spellbook in the pack. Actually, simplest robust rule: after parsing the character, if we know no spells, read the first available spellbook (on the ground or in the pack).\n\n2. **Cast in combat**: in `fight_heur.get_available_actions`, add a 'cast' action for the best known attack spell when a monster is in line of sight (using the same ray logic as `ranged_priority`), with a priority above melee.\n\nHmm, this is a decent chunk of work. Let me scope it:\n\nMinimal viable version:\n- `Character.parse_spellcast_view`: drop the `role not in (HEALER,)` guard. But careful: for a Healer, `known_spells` set to `{message: None}` when no popup. For a Wizard with no spells, the message is \"You don't know any spells.\" → `known_spells = {'You don't know any spells.': None}`. That's the existing fallback, fine. But better to make it robust: if the popup isn't the expected format, set known_spells = {}.\n\n- `Agent.read_spellbook` / a strategy to learn spells.\n- `fight_heur`: add cast actions.\n- `agent._fight2_perform_action`: handle the 'cast' action.\n\nAlso `agent.main()` currently comments out `parse_spellcast_view`. Need to call it (cheap, but costs a few turns; better to call lazily).\n\nLet me think about where to hook \"read a spellbook\". Options:\n(a) In `agent.main()` init: read a spellbook if one is in the inventory at start. Simple but only handles the start.\n(b) A general strategy in `global_strategy` preempt: \"if I know no attack spell and there's a spellbook on the ground or in the pack, go read it.\" More general.\n\nI'll do (b) but implemented so it's cheap to evaluate: only when `self.character.known_spells` has no usable attack spell.\n\nHmm, but the agent's `known_spells` must be initialized. Let me set `Character.known_spells = {}` and `spell_fail_chance = {}` in `__init__`.\n\nNow, how do we find spellbooks? `item.category == nh.SPBOOK_CLASS`. Let me check the Item class for helpers.\n\nNow about the **cast targeting**. Reuse the geometry in `ranged_priority`: walk from the agent along (dy,dx) until hitting a wall or a monster. If a monster is found in the ray and it's in our list, we can cast. `agent.cast` uses `calc_direction(..., allow_nonunit_distance=True)` to give a direction string for a long ray. Good — same as ranged.\n\nNow priorities. In `get_available_actions`, melee priority is 1 or 16. Ranged is 11. Let me give the cast a high priority when a monster is in the ray, e.g. 20, but only if we have enough energy. Also don't cast at a monster we don't want to fight — but the existing code already commits to fighting all visible monsters (melee actions are generated for all adjacent).\n\nAlso: force bolt has a fail chance. For a 0% fail it's a no-brainer.\n\nEnergy: `blstats.energy` (index 14) is current energy. force bolt cost = 1 (level 1 spell: `energycost = 3*(lev+1)/2`? hmm). Let me just require `energy >= 4` or use a small mapping. Let me actually measure the cost empirically... simpler: require energy >= 3.\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider: is casting really going to be a big win? Let's think about the *energy economy*:\n- Wizard max energy = 10 * (ulevel + 1). At XL1 that's 20. Observed 6/6 at start though! Hmm, \"Pw:6(6)\" — so max_energy is 6 at level 0? Hmm. Actually the observed snapshot had \"energy\": 6 at turn 1 and 20 at XL 2 (turn 403), 26 at XL 3, 32 at XL 4, 36 at XL 5, 51 at XL 6. Hmm, irregular. blstats[14] might not be energy...\n\nHold on: my snapshot used bl[14] for \"energy\" and the observed values were 6, 20, 26, 32, 36, 51. And the tty shows \"Pw:6(6)\" at start. Hmm, but blstats[14] = 6 matches. Then 20, 26, 32... Hmm, at XL2 it should be 20. Yes! max energy = 10*(ulevel+1): XL1=20, XL2=30... hmm observed 20 at xp2, 26 at xp3, 32 at xp4, 36 at xp5, 51 at xp6. Not a clean pattern, so bl[14] may be \"energy\" (current), which is capped at max.\n\nHmm, actually the BLStats namedtuple says: 'x y strength_percentage strength dexterity constitution intelligence wisdom charisma score hitpoints max_hitpoints depth gold energy max_energy armor_class monster_level experience_level experience_points time hunger_state ...'. So index 10=hitpoints, 11=max_hitpoints, 12=depth, 13=gold, 14=energy, 15=max_energy, 16=armor_class, 17=monster_level, 18=experience_level, 19=experience_points, 20=time, 21=hunger_state.\n\nMy snapshot printed hp from bl[10] and maxhp from bl[11] — correct. And energy bl[14]. At turn 1 energy=6, and tty showed Pw:6(6) → so max energy 6?? But bl[15] I didn't print. Hmm, at XL 2 energy=20. So max_energy at XL1 = 6? Hmm, that doesn't match 10*(lvl+1)=20.\n\nActually NetHack: `u.uen = u.uenmax = (u.ulevel + 1) * 10;` Hmm. But the tty showed Pw:6(6) at XP 1. Hmm, so max is 6. Hmm, maybe the wizard starts with reduced energy. Actually! NetHack: \"if (u.ulevel == 0) u.uenmax = 6\"? Hmm. Hmm, hmm: In `u_init.c`:\n```c\n    u.uen = u.uenmax = 6; /* ... */\n```\nHmm plausible for a fresh character. And energy regen: `regen = 20 * (u.ulevel + 1)`? At level 0 → 20/1000 per turn → 50 turns per point. Over 13,000 turns → 260 energy. Level 1 spell costs 1 energy... Actually let me look at NetHack's `energycost`:\n\n```c\nint\nenergycost(int spell)\n{\n    int energy;\n\n    energy = 3 * (spell_lev(spell) + 1) / 2; /* spells cost 3, 4, 5, ... */\n    ...\n    return energy;\n}\n```\nHmm, or in `spell.c`:\n```c\n/* energycost(spell) -- returns the energy cost of casting spell */\nint\nenergycost(int spell)\n{\n    int energy;\n\n    energy = 3 + 2 * spell_lev(spell);   /* ??? */\n    if (spell_lev(spell) > 1) ...\n    return energy;\n}\n```\nI don't remember. Let's just measure empirically in-game: cast and see the energy drop.\n\nLet me test the whole thing: read force bolt, find a monster, cast.\n\nOK, let me now write a probe that:\n1. Reads force bolt.\n2. Calls `parse_spellcast_view` with the guard removed (I'll temporarily patch).\n3. Walks around until a monster is adjacent, then casts.\n\nActually, faster: let me just implement the change in the code and test it on seeds. I'll write it carefully.\n\nBut before investing, let me sanity-check the potential by a quick experiment: patch the agent minimally to (a) read force bolt at start, (b) cast it whenever a monster is in the ray during fight2, and measure seeds 0 and 3.\n\nLet me look at Item/inventory API for reading books.\n\nLet me look at inventory.py.","time":{"start":1790680631241,"end":1790680650885}}}
