{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790680661332,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ece2685200194jpX0SyavP2qV","messageID":"msg_0ece260b8001pU4XxaRsz6GvTg","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"Good. Now let me write a prototype quickly and test on a couple of seeds. Let me implement:\n\n1. `Character.__init__`: `self.known_spells = {}`, `self.spell_fail_chance = {}`, `self.spells_parsed_at = -inf`.\n2. `Character.parse_spellcast_view()`: remove the HEALER guard, robust parsing.\n3. `Agent.read_spellbook(item)` — new primitive.\n4. `Agent.learn_spells()` strategy — preempt: if we know no attack spell and a spellbook is available (in pack or on the ground), read it.\n5. `fight_heur`: 'cast' actions.\n6. `agent._fight2_perform_action`: 'cast' handler.\n\nLet me check `utils` for `line_dis_from` and `inside`.\n\nLet me also decide: what determines \"we should cast\"? Let me keep it simple and robust:\n- We know at least one attack spell with fail chance <= some threshold.\n- We have enough energy.\n- A monster is in a clear ray.\n\nPriority: high (above ranged 11 and melee 16) — say 20. Because a spell at range both kills and avoids melee.\n\nHmm, but careful: casting at range-1 is a bad idea if the monster is adjacent (it can hit us while we cast). Actually force bolt in melee range: you can still cast it and it works. But given the wizard dies in melee, ranged is better.\n\nLet me also handle: the cast might be *wasted* if the monster moves out of the ray. But `get_priorities` recomputes each turn.\n\nAnother consideration: **the wizard is often not in `fight2` at all** because monsters are far. `fight2` triggers when monsters are within distance 7. Good — the ray geometry will find them.\n\nLet me now also think about whether to cast at *all* visible monsters. In the current design, `fight2` engages everything visible. So casting at anything in a ray is consistent.\n\nLet me write the code.\n\nOne more important thing: **reading a spellbook makes you blind** for a few turns. Need to make sure the bot doesn't get killed then. The bot should only read when it's reasonably safe (no adjacent hostile monsters) and then wait out the blindness (`wait_out_unexpected_state_strategy` already handles blind by waiting — good, it preempts on `character.prop.blind`).\n\nLet me now write the implementation.\n\nFirst let me check the `utils.py` helpers available in combat.","time":{"start":1790680655954,"end":1790680661319}}}
