{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790680691232,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ece28b66001VxyS0tntr0eP6d","messageID":"msg_0ece27d75001Y47sEMgXkR460Z","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"Now let me implement. I'll write the change with a `# hypothesis:` comment.\n\nFiles to change:\n1. `autoascend/character.py` — init known_spells, generalise `parse_spellcast_view`.\n2. `autoascend/agent.py` — add `read_spellbook`, add `learn_spells` strategy, handle 'cast' in `_fight2_perform_action`, call `parse_spellcast_view` after learning.\n3. `autoascend/combat/fight_heur.py` — add cast actions.\n4. `autoascend/global_logic.py` — hook the learn-spells strategy.\n\nLet me write it.\n\n### character.py\n\n```python\nATTACK_SPELLS = ['force bolt', 'magic missile', 'drain life', 'cone of cold', 'fireball', 'finger of death']\n```\n\n`parse_spellcast_view`:\n```python\n    def parse_spellcast_view(self):\n        self.known_spells = dict()\n        self.spell_fail_chance = dict()\n\n        with self.agent.atom_operation():\n            self.agent.step(A.Command.CAST)\n            popup = list(self.agent.popup)\n        if not popup:\n            return\n        if popup[0] != 'Choose which spell to cast' or not popup[1].startswith('Name'):\n            return\n        for line in popup[2:]:\n            matches = re.findall(...)\n            if len(matches) != 1:\n                continue\n            letter, spell_name, level, category, fail, retention = matches[0]\n            if len(letter) != 1:\n                continue\n            self.known_spells[spell_name] = letter\n            self.spell_fail_chance[spell_name] = int(fail) / 100\n        self.agent.step(A.Command.ESC)\n```\n\nCareful: the original code kept the ESC step outside the atom_operation. And it also handles the \"no popup\" case for Healer by `known_spells[message] = None`. I'll simplify: if no popup → known_spells stays empty. But the ESC must be sent when the menu is open. Note: `self.agent.step(A.Command.CAST)` inside `atom_operation` — after the atom operation ends, `update_state` is called. Then step ESC.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: the original `parse_spellcast_view` was used for Healer where `known_spells = {message: None}` for no-popup. `should_cast_heal` checks `'healing' in self.character.known_spells`. Removing that is fine because 'healing' wouldn't be in an empty dict.\n\nWait, careful: for the Healer role, when the cast view has a popup the original code parses it. When no popup, message = e.g. \"You don't know any spells.\" and it sets known_spells = {that message: None}. My version just leaves it empty. `should_cast_heal` then returns False. Same behaviour. Good.\n\nAlso: the ESC after the menu. In the original, ESC is only sent if a popup existed. Keep that.\n\nNow the level: `spell_lev` from the parsed `level` field. I need energy cost. Let me parse `level` and store it: `self.spell_level[spell_name] = int(level)`. That gives a principled energy cost model.\n\nNetHack `energycost`:\n```c\nint\nenergycost(int spell)\n{\n    int energy;\n\n    energy = 3 * (spell_lev(spell) + 1) / 2;   /* ??? */\n```\nLet me instead just be conservative: require `energy >= spell_level + 1` or something. Actually I measured: at 6/6 energy can I cast force bolt? Let me test empirically in the probe. Let me add a cast test.\n\n### agent.read_spellbook\n\n```python\n    def read_spellbook(self, item):\n        with self.atom_operation():\n            self.step(A.Command.READ)\n            if item in self.inventory.items.all_items:\n                self.type_text(self.inventory.items.get_letter(item))\n            else:\n                # on the ground: 'r' + letter also works for items below\n                self.type_text(self.inventory.items.get_letter(item))\n            for _ in range(4):\n                if 'Continue?' in self.message:\n                    self.type_text('y')\n                    break\n                if 'You begin to memorize' in self.message or 'difficult to comprehend' in self.message:\n                    break\n                self.type_text(' ')\n```\nHmm, the flow: 'r' → \"What do you want to read? [ijklm or ?*]\" → letter → either learned (\"You begin to memorize the runes.\") or \"This spellbook is difficult to comprehend. Continue? [yn] (n)\" → 'y' → \"You succeed...\" or fail.\n\nLet me look at the actual messages after reading force bolt (blessed, 0% fail): 'You begin to memorize the runes.  You stop studying.' — the \"You stop studying.\" is from my extra space.\n\nFor an uncursed book with fail chance, e.g. haste self at 91% fail: \"This spellbook is difficult to comprehend.  Continue? [yn] (n)\".\n\nLet me write:\n```python\n    def read_spellbook(self, item):\n        letter = self.inventory.items.get_letter(item)\n        learned = False\n        with self.atom_operation():\n            self.step(A.Command.READ)\n            self.type_text(letter)\n            for _ in range(4):\n                if 'Continue?' in self.message:\n                    self.type_text('y')\n                    continue\n                if 'memorize the runes' in self.message or \"don't seem to make sense\" in self.message:\n                    learned = True\n                    break\n                self.type_text(' ')\n        if learned:\n            self.character.parse_spellcast_view()\n        return learned\n```\nHmm — careful with infinite message accumulation: `self.message` accumulates during the atom operation. 'Continue?' would remain in the message after answering. But we break on the memorize check. Order: check 'memorize' first, then 'Continue?'.\n\nActually after answering 'y' to Continue?, the message is like \"You begin to memorize the runes.\" → next loop iteration catches it. Good.\n\nBut if the read fails entirely: \"You turn the pages, but the letters are unintelligible to you.\" Hmm. Let me break on any of several terminal strings.\n\nLet me simplify: run at most 4 iterations; break when the message contains 'memorize' or 'unintelligible' or \"don't seem to make sense\" or 'Continue?' after which we answer y once and then break.\n\nActually simplest robust version:\n\n```python\n        with self.atom_operation():\n            self.step(A.Command.READ)\n            self.type_text(letter)\n            for _ in range(4):\n                if 'Continue?' in self.message:\n                    self.type_text('y')\n                elif 'memorize the runes' in self.message or 'unintelligible' in self.message \\\n                        or \"don't seem to make sense\" in self.message:\n                    break\n                else:\n                    self.type_text(' ')\n```\nHmm, once we answer 'y', the message still contains 'Continue?' → infinite 'y' until 4 iterations. That's bounded, fine, but wastes turns. Let me track whether we already answered.\n\nLet me write it carefully with a flag.\n\n### The learn-spells strategy\n\n```python\n    @utils.debug_log('learn_spells')\n    @Strategy.wrap\n    def learn_spells(self):\n        if not self.character.known_spells:\n            self.character.parse_spellcast_view()   # cheap-ish; but costs turns\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, calling parse_spellcast_view every time is expensive. Let me gate: only parse if we have never parsed or we just read a book.\n\nSimpler and cheaper: \n- Keep `self.character.known_spells` updated only after reading a book (parse then).\n- The strategy triggers when: `not known_spells` and a spellbook is reachable (in inventory or on the ground).\n\nBut we also want the bot to pick up a spellbook it finds later. `gather_items` picks up SPBOOK (it's in the `categories` list in ItemPriority). So books will come into the pack. Then `learn_spells` reads the first unknown one.\n\nTo avoid re-reading the same book forever, track which books are already read: `self._read_spellbooks = set()` keyed by object name.\n\nLet me write:\n\n```python\n    @utils.debug_log('learn_spells')\n    @Strategy.wrap\n    def learn_spells(self):\n        if self.character.known_spells:\n            yield False\n        # a spellbook we can reach\n        target = None\n        for item in self.inventory.items:\n            if item.is_spellbook() and item.object.name not in self._read_spellbook_names:\n                target = item\n                break\n        if target is None:\n            for item in self.inventory.items_below_me:\n                if ...:\n                    target = item; on_ground = True\n        if target is None:\n            yield False\n        yield True\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, `item.is_spellbook()` doesn't exist. `item.category == nh.SPBOOK_CLASS`. And `item.object.name` for a spellbook gives e.g. 'force bolt'. Let me check O.Spellbook.\n\nLet me check the objects module for Spellbook names.\n\nAlso the \"don't learn while a monster is next to us\" safety: require no visible monster within a couple of squares (reading makes you blind). Let me add: `if any adjacent hostile monster: yield False`.\n\nAnd handle going to the ground book.\n\n### fight_heur cast actions\n\n```python\ndef cast_priority(agent, dy, dx, monsters):\n    \"\"\"Priority for casting a known attack spell along the ray (dy, dx).\"\"\"\n    spell = best_attack_spell(agent)\n    if spell is None:\n        return None\n    y, x = agent.blstats.y, agent.blstats.x\n    while True:\n        y += dy; x += dx\n        if not inside(agent, y, x):\n            return None\n        if agent.glyphs[y, x] in G.PETS or not agent.current_level().walkable[y, x]:\n            return None\n        if agent.glyphs[y, x] in G.MONS:\n            monster = [m for m in monsters if m[1] == y and m[2] == x]\n            if not monster:\n                return None\n            return 20, y, x, monster[0]\n```\n\nWait — `walkable[y,x]` is False for monsters? Let's check: in `update_level`, `level.walkable[mask & (level.objects == -1)] = True` for MONS/PETS/BODIES/OBJECTS/STATUES. And `level.objects` is only set for walkable floor cells... For a monster tile, `objects` stays -1 → walkable True. But `ranged_priority` uses the same check and it works for monsters. Actually hmm: in `ranged_priority` the check `if agent.glyphs[y, x] in G.PETS or not agent.current_level().walkable[y, x]: return None` comes BEFORE the MONS check. So for a monster tile, walkable must be True for ranged to work. Since ranged works in this bot (556 THROWs), walkable must be True on monster tiles. Good, I'll mirror it exactly.\n\nAlso energy check and fail-chance check.\n\n### best_attack_spell\n\n```python\nATTACK_SPELL_DAMAGE = {  # rough expected damage per cast, by spell name\n    'force bolt': ..., \n}\n```\nLet me keep it simple: prefer the known attack spell with the lowest fail chance and highest spell level? No — lowest level is cheaper and more reliable. Let me just prefer, among attack spells with fail chance <= 0.5, the one with the lowest fail chance, tie-break on highest spell level (more damage).\n\nHmm, simpler and safer: consider all known attack spells; pick the one minimizing `(fail_chance, -spell_level)`. And require energy >= cost.\n\nCost model: NetHack `energycost`: I'm fairly confident it is\n```c\nint\nenergycost(int spell)\n{\n    int energy;\n    energy = 3 * (spell_lev(spell) + 1) / 2;  /* 1 -> 3, 2 -> 4, ... */\n    ...\n}\n```\nLet me just measure. I'll measure by casting and looking at energy.\n\nLet me do that measurement now in a probe. Actually, simpler: implement with a conservative requirement and measure the actual energy delta in a probe. Let me measure.\n\nLet me write a quick probe: learn force bolt, then find a monster, cast, print energy before/after.\n\nLet me do it.\n\nActually — I realize I should double-check something first: how much time do I have / how expensive is this? I've used a fair amount already. The full 15-seed eval takes ~4 min with 8 parallel (but only 1 CPU, so ~4 min wall). That's fine. Let me move fast.\n\nLet me implement the change now and then measure.\n\nOne more design decision: where to call `learn_spells` from. In `global_logic.global_strategy()`, add a preempt before `fight2`:\n\n```python\n.preempt(self.agent, [\n    self.agent.learn_spells(),\n])\n```\n\nBut careful — preempt order: strategies are checked in order of the list within one `.preempt()` call? Let's look: `preempt` registers all strategies in `id2fun`, and `call_update_functions` iterates `self.on_update` in order. The strategies are added in list order, so earlier ones preempt first. In `global_strategy`, `.preempt(self.agent, [X, Y])` — X is checked first. The outermost preempt has the highest priority.\n\nOrder in global_strategy:\n1. solve_sokoban\n2. offer_corpses (+ eat_corpses_from_ground)\n3. wait_out_unexpected_state\n4. cure_disease\n5. eat_corpses\n6. follow_guard\n7. fight2\n8. engulfed_fight\n9. emergency_strategy\n\nSo adding learn_spells after (say) eat_corpses and before follow_guard, or right before fight2. Actually, we want it to happen early — before fighting. But it also must not interrupt a fight. Let me put it right after `wait_out_unexpected_state` (so blindness is handled) and before `eat_corpses`... hmm, actually the safety condition (no adjacent monsters) protects us.\n\nHmm, but the *first* thing is: at game start, the wizard has a spellbook in her pack. `learn_spells` would fire immediately at turn ~5 and read it. \n\nBut careful: `wait_out_unexpected_state_strategy` preempts on blind. Reading makes you blind → that strategy will make us wait. Good.\n\nLet me place `learn_spells` just before `fight2` in the list so it doesn't interfere with food/emergency. Actually it's higher priority than fight2 in the list... The list is [sokoban, offer_corpses, wait_out, cure_disease, eat, follow_guard, fight2, engulfed, emergency]. Earlier in the list = higher priority. So if I insert learn_spells at index 5 (before follow_guard), it preempts follow_guard and fight2. With the \"no adjacent monster\" safety condition, it will effectively only fire when it's safe. \n\nNow let's write the code.\n\nLet me first check O.Spellbook / object names.","time":{"start":1790680664934,"end":1790680691225}}}
