{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790680923623,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ece61ebf001Caj9sukoBQUz3y","messageID":"msg_0ece6182e001Ex39f3eRDXgoYJ","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting: `asked=False` and empty message. The first attempt's `atom_operation` steps: `A.Command.CAST` then typing the letter. But `type_text` may have consumed... hmm, message is ''. The spell wasn't cast at all.\n\nHmm, the atom_operation ended with message ''. That's because after the loop the last action was a space which cleared the message? No — `self.message` is the raw message, `single_message` is the new one. In `agent.update`, `self.message` gets replaced. Actually in this codebase `self.message` is the full message buffer and `self.single_message` is new text. The existing code checks `self.message` for \"In what direction?\" — that's the raw tty message, which may lag.\n\nMy probe is using `ag.message` which is the message buffer. After typing spaces the buffer shows \"\". Hmm, in the earlier probe3, `cast` worked and the message showed properly.\n\nThe issue in probe4: the wizard is *still blind/studying* (\"You stop studying.\") — no. Hmm, `asked=False` and message ''.\n\nHmm, actually maybe the CAST command didn't open the popup because... Let me use `ag.popup` and `ag.single_message` to be accurate. Actually, let me not go down this rabbit hole in a hand-written probe: the important finding is from the real run — \"The spell hits the kobold! You kill the kobold!\" appeared twice, and \"The spell hits the newt! You kill the newt!\". So casting works in the real bot.\n\nThe real bug to fix is the **energy stall**: 193 \"You don't have enough energy to cast that spell.\" followed by 200-step inactivity assertion.\n\nSo: `can_cast` says energy >= 3 but the game refuses. Two possible causes:\n(a) force bolt's true energy cost is > 6 (the starting energy), or\n(b) energy regen timing.\n\nHmm, in the diag run, casts DID happen (2 kills by spell). Then energy ran out and it stalled.\n\nThe cleanest fix: after a failed cast (no \"In what direction?\"), remember a cooldown; and make the stall impossible. But the deeper issue is the bot burning its whole turn budget retrying a cast that can't happen, with zero progress → inactivity assertion → episode ends at turn 848.\n\nActually wait. Why zero progress? Each cast attempt DOES consume actions: `self.step(A.Command.CAST)` (1 turn), typing the letter (1 turn), 3 spaces (each is a turn, \"Unknown command\" — actually an unknown command costs 0 turns in NetHack? It says \"Unknown command\" which doesn't advance a turn). So the fight2 loop spins: perform action → no turn passes → recompute → same action → ... → 200 steps.\n\nHmm, but `get_priorities` IS recomputed each iteration of fight2's loop (since the error occurred after 200 `update` calls in the same fight2). And each recompute re-offers 'cast' since `can_cast` is true (energy still >= 3 in blstats, because blstats only updates on a turn... and no turn passes, so blstats is stale!).\n\nThat's it: **stale blstats**. Energy is really 0, but blstats says 6 (or whatever) because no turn advanced, so the decision repeats forever.\n\nWait, but energy 6 at the start. If we cast (cost 3), energy 3, cast, energy 0. Then \"not enough energy\". blstats says 0 then. Then can_cast → 0 >= 3 false. Hmm.\n\nUnless... `self.blstats` is only updated in `update()` on turn changes, and `get_priorities` is called from `fight2` after `self.update()`. Hmm.\n\nOK, rather than theorize, let me instrument. Let me add a temporary debug: print energy + best spell each time the cast action is performed. Actually simpler: let me just look at the diag's action histogram to see if CAST appears, and add an energy log.\n\nAlternative pragmatic approach: make the cast action robust by construction:\n1. Require a *bigger* energy margin so the cast can't fail for energy: e.g. `energy >= 2*level + 1` is 3; instead require `energy > 2*level + 1` (i.e., 4+) — still could fail.\n2. Add a failure cooldown: `last_cast_fail_turn` exists in agent and is used by healer (`should_cast_heal` checks `self.agent._last_turn - self.agent.last_cast_fail_turn[spell] < 5`). Let me use the same guard in `cast_priority`: don't offer a cast within N turns of a failed cast for that spell. That directly prevents the tight loop: the first failure sets the fail turn, and for the next 5 turns the cast is not offered.\n\nThat's a clean, principled fix using existing machinery. Let me also log it.\n\nBut 5 turns might be too short if energy regen is that slow. With `energy >= 3` required and a failed cast meaning energy < 3, regen +1 per 2 turns → 6 turns to get 3 energy. So a cooldown of, say, 10 turns is safer. Hmm, but the healer's 5 was for a different reason.\n\nLet me be more careful and derive the real energy cost empirically. Let me fix probe4 to use `single_message` and check properly. Actually — the cleanest measurement: in the real run, log energy before/after successful casts. Let me add a temporary log to the diag script... \n\nActually, simplest robust design: **require energy >= 2 * level + 1, and after a failed cast, block that spell for 15 turns.** And additionally make `can_cast` require a margin of 1 above the estimated cost: `energy > spell_energy_cost` (strictly greater) — since a cast that leaves 0 energy with a failure is what stalls.\n\nHmm, but the stall isn't from the cast being at 0 energy per se; the stall is from retrying without progress. The cooldown fixes that regardless of the cost. Let me implement the cooldown and re-run; if casts still fail for energy, the bot will simply not cast (it falls through to melee) and the run continues.\n\nLet me also reconsider: is the *stall* even new? In the baseline, does the bot ever stall? The assertion is a general safety net; baseline never hit it. So my change introduced it. The cooldown will fix it.\n\nLet me implement:\n- In `Character.can_cast`: keep energy check.\n- In `cast_priority`: skip if `agent._last_turn - agent.last_cast_fail_turn[spell] < 15`.\n\nWait, `last_cast_fail_turn` is keyed by spell name; `cast()` sets it on failure. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: if the cast never even gets to the direction prompt because of energy, `agent.cast` records `last_cast_fail_turn[spell] = self._last_turn`. Good.\n\nAlso, `agent.cast` sends up to 3 spaces which produce \"Unknown command\" messages — harmless but let me not send them when the cast was rejected. Actually the loop breaks only on 'In what direction?'; it should also break if the cast was rejected. Let me add a check for 'You don't have enough energy' / 'You fail to cast' / 'You cannot cast' to break early. That reduces wasted actions.\n\nLet me rewrite the `type_letters` generator:\n```python\n            def type_letters():\n                if 'You are too impaired' in self.message:\n                    return\n                yield self.character.known_spells[spell_name]\n                for _ in range(3):\n                    if 'In what direction?' in self.message or CAST_REFUSED in self.message:\n                        break\n                    yield ' '\n                ...\n```\nHmm, but `self.message` is the whole buffer which may contain stale text from earlier messages (the buffer accumulates). That's dangerous: 'You don't have enough energy to cast that spell.' from a previous failed cast would linger in the buffer and break immediately. Actually the buffer is reset each turn in NLE? `self.message` is set from the tty message each update; messages accumulate within a turn but the buffer is cleared on... Let me check how the existing code handles this: `should_cast_heal` uses `self.message` for \"You fail to cast heal\". And `agent.update` sets `self.message = observation['message']` which is the *full* tty message for the current turn (NLE returns the whole tty message string, which is typically just the current turn's messages since the tty is flushed per turn).\n\nActually in NLE, `message` is the current message buffer content. Hmm, in the earlier diag output, msg_hist showed distinct messages, so the buffer gets cleared.\n\nLet me use a robust check: look for the specific refusal in `self.message` but ALSO require that the message is not just stale. Simpler: break on 'In what direction?' or on any of the known refusal strings, and clear risk is low because after a successful cast the buffer shows the combat result.\n\nHmm, actually there's a cleaner approach: check `self.single_message` (only the *new* message this turn). Let me check whether `single_message` is set in `update`. Yes — `self.single_message` is set from the diff of the message. But `type_letters` yields multiple actions, and by the time we check, the message from the CAST command step is the \"new\" one. Using `single_message` is risky because the next action clears it.\n\nI'll go with the cooldown as the primary safety (it doesn't depend on message parsing) plus a light break condition. Let me implement and test.\n\nLet me also double-check: `self._last_turn` is set in update. And `last_cast_fail_turn` is a defaultdict with -inf. Good.\n\nNow, one more consideration: should I also reduce the *pri* when the monster is adjacent (melee at 16 is lower number = better? Let me check: `actions.append((pri, action))` and then sorted? In `_fight2_perform_action`, `best_action = sorted(self.attack_queue)[:1]`? Let me check how the best action is chosen — likely `min` or sorted ascending. ranged_priority returns 11 for distance>1 and 13 for distance 1; melee returns 1/16. And in get_priorities: `if pri > 1000: continue`. And `actions.sort(key=lambda x: -x[0])`? Let me look.\n\nLet me read the relevant part of fight_heur and agent's fight2.","time":{"start":1790680899263,"end":1790680923616}}}
