{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790681685722,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ecf1fefd001ixqQQ2NJjAzeYz","messageID":"msg_0ecf1fb39001mlNpzgrS5htF75","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"Now with the correct paired evaluation: mean 0.0612 vs 0.0624 — a wash. 5 forward, 7 backward.\n\nSo the change is roughly neutral overall, with huge variance. Some seeds improve enormously (3: +0.054, 13: +0.050, 14: +0.043, 1: +0.038, 10: +0.027) and others regress (4: -0.062, 8: -0.050, 11: -0.043, 2: -0.038).\n\nThe regressions look like runs that die much earlier. The wins are runs that survive much longer.\n\nSo the spell is a genuine improvement in *capability*, but the aggressive priority-20 casting hurts in some situations. Key hypothesis: casting every time starves the wizard of energy AND makes it ignore better options, plus the \"You don't have enough energy\" churn wastes steps.\n\nKey improvements to consider:\n1. Only cast when the target is NOT adjacent (ranged advantage), letting melee handle point-blank. Force bolt at level 1 does 1d6+1; a hit-and-run isn't available here. Hmm.\n2. Only cast when the monster is at distance >= 2.\n3. Keep energy in reserve for emergency (e.g. require energy > cost + margin).\n4. Reduce the wasted \"Unknown command ' '\" steps.\n\nBut hold on — I should reconsider whether the *learning* or the *casting* is the source of the regressions. A cheaper, more focused variant: **only cast when the monster is not adjacent** (i.e., use the spell as a ranged weapon and keep melee for melee). That's the classic \"kite with spells\" pattern and it minimizes disruption to the existing (working) melee behavior.\n\nLet me look at the data more carefully to decide. Let me examine one regression closely — seed 8, which died at 4331 turns vs 23604 baseline, killed by a jackal. A jackal is a weak, fast monster. If the wizard casts at a jackal at range 1 and fails/uses energy, it dies. Hmm, but force bolt has 0% fail.\n\nHmm! Wait. Maybe the real problem: **energy**. The wizard's energy pool is small. Casting 3 energy per bolt means the wizard can cast only a couple of times, then must rest to regenerate. Resting costs turns. And \"You don't have enough energy\" means `can_cast` (energy > 3) is sometimes true but the real cost is higher. Let me MEASURE the real energy cost of force bolt properly. If the real cost is e.g. 6 (max energy at XP1 = 6, so `energy > 3` is true at 6 but the cast is rejected), then my gate is wrong and the bot wastes a lot of steps/turns trying.\n\nThat's a concrete, testable question. Let me measure it correctly with a proper probe that uses `single_message`/popup.\n\nLet me write a careful probe: wait until not blind, then find a monster, record energy, cast, print energy after and the message. Use the real `agent.cast`.\n\nActually simpler: I can measure the *rejection threshold* by draining energy with the `rest` command and trying to cast at each energy level. Even simpler: cast successfully once and print the energy delta. Let me do that — but the earlier probe failed to get the direction prompt. Why? Because the wizard was still \"studying\"/blind? The message was '' and asked=False.\n\nHmm, in probe3 the cast worked (with the real `ag.cast`). In probe4 I hand-rolled the flow and it failed. The difference: probe3 used `ag.cast('force bolt', dir)`. And it printed \"The spell hits the kobold! You kill the kobold!\". But I never printed the energy delta there.\n\nLet me redo probe3 with energy printing. Modify probe3 to print energy before/after.","time":{"start":1790681677565,"end":1790681685718}}}
