{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1790681775038,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ecf37bba0014zM1SCO24fOrqP","messageID":"msg_0ecf37710001pPxcbKcanq0FlV","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790681793506,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ecf37bbc0010uYOACZwILmW5V","messageID":"msg_0ecf37710001pPxcbKcanq0FlV","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"Very telling. The candidate's energy is often near 0 (3, 9) while the baseline sits at max (20, 26). So the wizard is constantly energy-starved from casting, and the extra turns spent regenerating energy are turns not spent eating.\n\nSo: casting 5 energy per bolt is a *real* ongoing cost. The question is whether the XP gained per turn compensates. Empirically across 15 seeds: no (5F/7B, mean -0.001).\n\nRefinements to try (in order of expected value):\n\n**A. Only cast at range >= 2.** Preserves melee; the spell adds ranged damage. Less energy spent overall.\n\n**B. Gate the cast on a comfortable energy margin** so the wizard never drops to 0 energy (keeping energy for the emergency, and avoiding the wasted attempts). E.g. require `energy > cost + 4`.\n\n**C. Don't cast at weak monsters** where the staff is fine? No — the spell never misses, so it's better.\n\nLet me do A+B with the corrected cost, which is a coherent single idea: \"use the spell as a ranged weapon, and only when the energy economy allows.\"\n\nImplementation:\n- `spell_energy_cost`: use the measured NetHack 3.6 relationship. I need a defensible formula. Let me reason from what I know: level 1 → 5. NetHack 3.6's actual code (from `src/spell.c`):\n\n```c\n/* Returns energy cost of casting spell */\nint\nenergycost(int spell)\n{\n    int energy;\n\n    energy = 3 + 2 * (spell_lev(spell) - 1);   /* 3, 5, 7, ... */\n    energy += 2;   /* ??? */\n```\nHmm, if energy = 3 + 2*(lev-1) then level 1 = 3, level 2 = 5, level 3 = 7. If level 1 = 5, maybe the formula is `5 + 2*(lev-1)`? Or `3*(lev)`? level 1 → 3. No.\n\nHmm! Wait. Maybe the cost isn't from the spell but from `drain_energy` for the *knowledge*? No.\n\nOK here's a cleaner idea that avoids the formula entirely and is arguably *better engineering*: **make the gate empirical and self-correcting.**\n\n```python\n# The wizard starts each game with 6 energy and force bolt costs 5, so the cost\n# model has to be conservative; learn it from the game instead of guessing.\nself.spell_energy_cost_observed = defaultdict(lambda: 0)  # in Agent\n```\nand in `Character.spell_energy_cost(name)`:\n```python\n    observed = self.agent.spell_energy_cost_observed.get(name)\n    if observed:\n        return observed\n    return 2 * self.spell_levels.get(name, 1) + 3   # starting guess\n```\nand in `cast()`, after the attempt, record the observed cost: if the cast succeeded and we saw the energy drop, `observed[name] = max(observed.get(name,0), e0 - e1)`; if it was refused at energy e, `observed[name] = max(..., e + 1)`.\n\nThat's self-tuning but more code. Hmm, is it justified? It's ~8 lines and removes a guess that I know is wrong. I think it's worth it, but it adds statefulness that could interact badly.\n\nLet me simplify: since I can *measure* force bolt = 5 and the bot only ever has force bolt (its only book is force bolt, and it's the only spell it will realistically use early), a simple conservative linear model anchored on the measurement is fine and honest:\n\n```python\n    def spell_energy_cost(self, spell_name):\n        # Measured in-game: a level 1 spell costs 5 energy, and a wiz starts the\n        # game with 6, so this budget is deliberately pessimistic. Over-estimating\n        # only makes the wizard melee a little more; under-estimating makes it\n        # queue up casts the game will refuse, which cost it whole turns.\n        return 5 + 2 * (self.spell_levels.get(spell_name, 1) - 1)\n```\n\nHmm, \"measured in-game: level 1 costs 5\" — I can state that as the comment, and note the linear extrapolation. That's defensible.\n\nAnd then `can_cast` = `energy > cost + margin`. With margin 0, at energy 6 the wizard can cast (5). At energy 5 it can't. Good, that matches the game's own behavior exactly for level 1. No wasted attempts! That's the key fix.\n\nSo: `can_cast`: `energy > spell_energy_cost(name)`. With cost 5 and energy 6 → 6 > 5 → True. Energy 5 → False. The game requires energy >= 5 to cast. So my gate (energy > 5) is slightly stricter (requires 6). That's a 1-energy safety margin, which prevents the boundary case.\n\n**Now, do I also need change A (range >= 2)?** Let me test A separately, but each full eval is ~4-5 minutes, so I can afford a few.\n\nPriority: first fix the cost model (that's a clear bug), keep priority 20, evaluate. Then try range>=2, evaluate. Pick the winner.\n\nLet me make the cost fix now and run the eval.","time":{"start":1790681775036,"end":1790681793500}}}
