{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790682089538,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ecf7f1bd001ZPBYH2B3XQ5JIL","messageID":"msg_0ecf7ec92001XdyL7JDrpAPxkj","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"Worse: 0.0546, 6F/9B. The corrected energy gate made it *worse*. Hmm, that's surprising.\n\nWait — with the corrected gate, the wizard casts *less* often (only when energy > 5). Hmm, but the result got worse. That contradicts the \"energy waste\" theory... or the variance is just enormous.\n\nLook at the variance: seed 0 went from 8052 turns (bad run) to 20594 turns (great). Seed 7: 3845 turns. These swings are chaotic — small behavioral changes cascade into completely different runs.\n\nThis tells me the per-seed variance is so large (death by random monster) that 15 seeds can't reliably distinguish variants. Mean over 15 seeds has a std of roughly... deltas range ±0.1, so the standard error is ~0.025/√15 ≈ 0.006. The observed differences (0.0624 vs 0.0546 = 0.008) are within noise.\n\nSo I'm in a regime where the measurement is too noisy to tune. I need a *bigger* effect or a *less noisy* metric.\n\nLet me think differently. What is the biggest lever? Looking at the baseline table: many seeds die to random monsters at various levels. The wizard is a fragile 12-HP character with an unskilled staff.\n\nHmm, but the task says: make ONE focused change. The hypothesis-driven approach. Let me reconsider what's the most reliable, high-impact, low-variance improvement.\n\nKey observation from the data: **the wizard is dying of starvation repeatedly** (seed 3 \"died of starvation\", seed 6 gas spore after fainting, seed 5 newt). And 17 faints on seed 0. Starvation seems to be a recurring, systematic failure mode. If the wizard reliably ate, it would survive much longer.\n\nBut careful — that's a second hypothesis, and I already have a spell-based one. The instruction was to pick one. Given the noise, though, I should pick the change with the best *expected* effect and lowest variance.\n\nLet me reconsider the spell idea more carefully. Is it plausible that casting *hurts*? Yes:\n- 5 energy per cast. Energy regen is ~1 per 2 turns, so a cast costs ~10 turns of rest. In 10 turns a fighter would kill ~0 monsters... hmm, actually the baseline gets 190 kills in 13697 turns = 1 kill per 72 turns. So spending 10 turns to deal 4.5 damage is terrible compared to just swinging!\n\nWait, that changes everything. The baseline's kill rate is 72 turns/kill — that's very slow, mostly searching. The energy cost of a cast is ~5 energy ≈ 10 turns of regen. A force bolt does 1d6+1 ≈ 4.5 damage. A newt has ~4 HP. So one cast kills a newt, cost ~10 turns of regen. Baseline kills a newt in ~72 turns. So a cast is 7x more energy-efficient than the baseline's approach... but the wizard still needs to *find* monsters.\n\nHmm, so casting should be a clear win in energy terms. Unless the regen blocks the wizard from doing anything else (it can't eat while... no, it can).\n\nThe real problem: while the wizard regenerates energy it's not killing. But it also wasn't killing much before.\n\nOK. Given the noise, let me consider a fundamentally more reliable change. Let me look at the data again for what kills the wizard most often across the 15 baseline seeds:\n- goblin, orcish arrow (poison), grid bug, hobbit, hobgoblin, giant rat, gnome, jackal, bat, fox, werejackal, killer bee, gray unicorn, rothe, shuriken, gas spore, wand...\n\nThe baseline dies to *random weak monsters* constantly. HP is 12-46. The wizard has no armor (AC 10) and an unskilled staff.\n\nThe single most reliable survival improvement for a level-1 wizard: **more HP / better AC**... but that's not \"focused\" either.\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider the task framing. The task is from a benchmark: \"make one focused change that raises the score\". The score is the mean over seeds. I should aim for the highest mean with the best F/B ratio. I've got 0.0624 baseline. cand2 (cost 3, priority 20) got 0.0612, 5F/7B. cand3 (cost 5) got 0.0546, 6F/9B.\n\nNeither wins. The noise is brutal. Let me get a *low-variance* signal: instead of the final progress, measure something more stable like **turns survived** or **XP rate**, or better: run more seeds. I could evaluate on 30-60 seeds to reduce noise. The arena allows arbitrary seeds 0-14 for \"public\"... but the published score is only over 0-14. Still, using more seeds gives a better estimate of the true effect, and if the true effect is positive it should also help on 0-14 in expectation.\n\nGiven 1 CPU and ~4.5 min for 15 seeds, 45 seeds would be ~13 min. That's affordable and would cut the standard error by √3.\n\nLet me do that: evaluate the baseline and the candidate on seeds 0-44 to get a reliable signal. That's ~26 min of compute for both. Worth it.\n\nActually, better plan: I have limited time/compute. Let me be strategic:\n1. Run the BASELINE on seeds 15-44 (30 new seeds) to extend the baseline reference: ~9 min.\n2. Run the CANDIDATE (cand2 config, the better one) on seeds 15-44: ~9 min.\n3. Compare on 45 seeds total → much tighter error bars.\n\nBut wait, I need to be careful: `--secret public` may only allow specific seeds. Let me just try a batch with seeds 15-44.\n\nAlso, before burning that compute, let me settle the design. Current state: the change is neutral-to-slightly-negative. I need a variant with a clearly positive mechanism.\n\nLet me think about the *mechanism* more rigorously using the low-variance signal I have: **turns survived** and **kills**.\n\nFrom cand2 (cost-3 version) vs baseline, per seed turns:\nseed 0: 21789 vs 13697 (+)\n1: 21402 vs 17693 (+)\n2: 11215 vs 20700 (-)\n3: 20817 vs 3052 (+++)\n4: 24395 vs 32346 (-)\n5: 15198 vs 14486 (+)\n6: 2557 vs 11919 (---)\n7: 25721 vs 23971 (+)\n8: 25721? no 25721 is seed7. seed8: 4331 vs 23604 (---)\n9: 11619 vs 24295 (-)\n10: 20209 vs 4014 (+++)\n11: 19221 vs 27094 (-)\n12: 6337 vs 12509 (-)\n13: 27785 vs 5206 (+++)\n14: 28696 vs 22485 (+)\n\nTurns: 6 seeds much better, 6 much worse, 3 same. Exactly balanced. High variance, zero mean effect. The spell changes the trajectory so much that deaths are essentially re-rolled.\n\nThis tells me: **any change that alters the combat trajectory will re-roll deaths and give ~0 mean effect with huge variance**, unless the mechanism is strong.\n\nSo the winning move is to find a change with a *systematic* effect. Two candidates:\n\n(a) **Starvation**: multiple seeds die of starvation/fainting. Systematic, high leverage. But is it in the \"one focused change\" spirit? Yes — it's a focused change.\n\n(b) **Learn more spells / cast more efficiently**: marginal.\n\nLet me quantify (a) properly: how many baseline deaths involve hunger? From the baseline arena JSON I only have cause_of_death. Let me check: seed deaths in the *candidate* runs mentioned \"died of starvation\" and gas spore. Let me count in the baseline.\n\nLet me get the baseline causes of death.\n\nActually, I realize I should question my earlier conclusion. The user asked me to make one focused change. I've been at this a while. Let me get the data I need efficiently:\n\n1. Get the baseline causes of death for all 15 seeds (from baseline.json) — free.\n2. Run a bigger paired evaluation (30-45 seeds) for the best candidate to see if the effect is real.\n\nLet me do (1) now and also start (2).\n\nActually, thinking about the noise problem more: the arena's per-seed score is dominated by *when the wizard dies*, which is a chaotic function. With 15 seeds, I literally cannot measure a small effect. So the responsible thing is to evaluate on many more seeds. Let me check that seeds beyond 14 are allowed.\n\nLet me look at the seeds module for the allowed range.","time":{"start":1790682067389,"end":1790682089532}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1790682089988,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_jtcuhp38pb2n_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /opt/kit/nethackers/arena && sed -n '1,80p' seeds.py 2>/dev/null || rg -n \"def trajectory_spec|seed\" seeds.py | head -30"},"output":"from __future__ import annotations\n\nimport hashlib\nimport hmac\n\nfrom nethackers.contracts.models import TrajectorySpec\n\n_MAX_SEED = (1 << 63) - 1\n\n\ndef secret_fingerprint(secret: str) -> str:\n    return hashlib.sha256(secret.encode()).hexdigest()\n\n\ndef _seed_part(digest: bytes, offset: int) -> int:\n    return int.from_bytes(digest[offset : offset + 8], \"big\") & _MAX_SEED\n\n\ndef trajectory_spec(secret: str, evaluation_id: str, trajectory_id: int) -> TrajectorySpec:\n    if not secret:\n        raise ValueError(\"evaluation secret must not be empty\")\n    if trajectory_id < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"trajectory_id must be non-negative\")\n    message = f\"nethack-arena\\0{evaluation_id}\\0{trajectory_id}\".encode()\n    digest = hmac.new(secret.encode(), message, hashlib.sha256).digest()\n    return TrajectorySpec(\n        trajectory_id=trajectory_id,\n        core_seed=_seed_part(digest, 0),\n        display_seed=_seed_part(digest, 8),\n        level_seed=_seed_part(digest, 16),\n        bot_seed=_seed_part(digest, 24),\n    )\n\n\ndef trajectory_specs(\n    secret: str,\n    evaluation_id: str,\n    count: int,\n) -> tuple[TrajectorySpec, ...]:\n    if count <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"trajectory count must be positive\")\n    return tuple(\n        trajectory_spec(secret, evaluation_id, trajectory_id)\n        for trajectory_id in range(count)\n    )\n","metadata":{"output":"from __future__ import annotations\n\nimport hashlib\nimport hmac\n\nfrom nethackers.contracts.models import TrajectorySpec\n\n_MAX_SEED = (1 << 63) - 1\n\n\ndef secret_fingerprint(secret: str) -> str:\n    return hashlib.sha256(secret.encode()).hexdigest()\n\n\ndef _seed_part(digest: bytes, offset: int) -> int:\n    return int.from_bytes(digest[offset : offset + 8], \"big\") & _MAX_SEED\n\n\ndef trajectory_spec(secret: str, evaluation_id: str, trajectory_id: int) -> TrajectorySpec:\n    if not secret:\n        raise ValueError(\"evaluation secret must not be empty\")\n    if trajectory_id < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"trajectory_id must be non-negative\")\n    message = f\"nethack-arena\\0{evaluation_id}\\0{trajectory_id}\".encode()\n    digest = hmac.new(secret.encode(), message, hashlib.sha256).digest()\n    return TrajectorySpec(\n        trajectory_id=trajectory_id,\n        core_seed=_seed_part(digest, 0),\n        display_seed=_seed_part(digest, 8),\n        level_seed=_seed_part(digest, 16),\n        bot_seed=_seed_part(digest, 24),\n    )\n\n\ndef trajectory_specs(\n    secret: str,\n    evaluation_id: str,\n    count: int,\n) -> tuple[TrajectorySpec, ...]:\n    if count <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"trajectory count must be positive\")\n    return tuple(\n        trajectory_spec(secret, evaluation_id, trajectory_id)\n        for trajectory_id in range(count)\n    )\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /opt/kit/nethackers/arena && sed -n '1,80p' seeds.py 2>/dev/null || rg -n \"def trajectory_spec|seed\" seeds.py | head -30","time":{"start":1790682089969,"end":1790682089986}},"id":"prt_0ecf84950001dNdmocib51IwWS","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","messageID":"msg_0ecf7ec92001XdyL7JDrpAPxkj"}}
