{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790682105158,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ecf8560b001eoXLdIJpHAHEe4","messageID":"msg_0ecf84a1b001CMPziQw7EeKnMB","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"Confirmed: `trajectory_spec` includes the `evaluation_id` in the HMAC, so **any evaluation_id other than \"local\" produces entirely different seeds**. That explains the cand1 discrepancy. Critical: always use `--evaluation-id local --secret public` to match the published baseline.\n\nArbitrary trajectory_ids are allowed. So I can evaluate on seeds 0-44 (or more) for a lower-variance estimate.\n\nLet me now:\n1. Build a 45-seed batch (0-44).\n2. Run the baseline (stash my changes) and the candidate.\n\nBut running the baseline requires reverting my changes. Let me use git — is /workspace a git repo? Earlier the env said \"Is directory a git repo: no\". So no git. I need to back up my modified files and restore the originals.\n\nI don't have the originals... but I can reconstruct them: my edits were surgical and I know exactly what they were. Safer: copy the current modified files aside, then revert each edit by reversing it. Risky.\n\nBetter approach: create a *copy* of the whole /workspace tree as the baseline solution dir with the original files restored. But I don't have the originals.\n\nHmm. Alternative: I already HAVE the baseline results for seeds 0-14. For more seeds I need the original code.\n\nCleanest: reconstruct the original files by reversing my edits. My edits were:\n1. agent.py: cast() (added return, allow_nonunit_distance, _direction_action), added _direction_action static method + direction refactor, added `self._read_spellbooks = set()`, added read_spellbook + learn_spells, added the 'cast' branch in _fight2_perform_action, added the hypothesis comment.\n2. character.py: added ATTACK_SPELL_DAMAGE, added 3 dict initializers, rewrote parse_spellcast_view, added spell_energy_cost/can_cast/best_attack_spell.\n3. fight_heur.py: added cast_priority, the cast action block, changed the 'melee','ranged' tuple to include 'cast', added the Character import.\n4. global_logic.py: added the learn_spells preempt.\n5. stats_logger.py: added 2 keys.\n6. combat/utils.py: added the 'cast' action_str branch.\n\nThat's a lot to reverse by hand, but I did a \"read\" of each edited region so I have the original text in the transcript for most.\n\nMUCH simpler approach: make a baseline copy of the repo where I *disable* my change via a single flag. E.g., add an env-var/constant switch `USE_SPELLS = False` in the code and run the baseline that way. But the arena's max_steps/steps accounting is identical, and behavior would be exactly the original as long as the switch short-circuits everything:\n- `learn_spells` returns immediately\n- `cast_priority` returns None\n- `spell_levels`/known_spells are only populated by read_spellbook → not called.\n\nThat's a faithful baseline: the only difference from the original would be... `parse_spellcast_view` is no longer called anywhere at init (it wasn't before either, for wizards), `Agent.cast` changed (unused in baseline), `_direction_action` refactor (behaviorally identical), the 'cast' action_str (never triggered), stats_logger keys (inert). So yes, with the switch off, the code is behaviorally identical to the original baseline.\n\nThat's a clean, low-risk way to A/B on 45+ seeds. And it's a temporary dev switch I must remove before finishing.\n\nLet me implement: a module-level flag in autoascend/character.py? Better in a neutral place. I'll put it in `autoascend/agent.py`? Circular import concerns... Let me put it in `autoascend/character.py` as `SPELLS_ENABLED = os.environ.get('AUTOASCEND_SPELLS', '1') != '0'`. Hmm, autoascend/character.py imports os? Let me just read the env in each place. Simpler: define in `autoascend/utils.py`? fight_heur imports `..utils`, agent imports `utils`, character imports... let me check.\n\nSimplest and least invasive: use an env var read at the two decision points:\n- `Character.best_attack_spell()`: return None if disabled.\n- `Agent.learn_spells()`: yield False if disabled.\n\nThen everything downstream is inert. \n\nLet me add a tiny helper in character.py:\n```python\ndef spells_enabled():\n    return os.environ.get('AUTOASCEND_SPELLS', '1') != '0'\n```\nand use it in both places. character.py already imports os? Let me check. If not, add it.\n\nThen run: baseline = AUTOASCEND_SPELLS=0, candidate = 1, over 45 seeds, same process invocation. That gives a paired comparison with tight error bars. ~27 min for both (45 seeds × ~25s / 8 parallel on 1 CPU... earlier 15 seeds took 4.2 min wall with 8 parallel on presumably more cores; user time was 12m56 for 4m11 wall → ~3x parallelism. So 45 seeds ≈ 12.6 min per variant, 25 min for both).\n\nThat's a lot of wall time but it's the right call — otherwise I can't tell signal from noise.\n\nLet me do it. First the switch, then launch the 45-seed baseline, then the 45-seed candidate.\n\nActually, to be efficient: run both in ONE background job sequentially, then analyze. Let me use nohup and poll.\n\nLet me set it up.","time":{"start":1790682093067,"end":1790682105151}}}
