{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1790682143616,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ecf91b6e0018aw4t11q8i5B6U","messageID":"msg_0ecf91547001kCYsqcAvMEQEpu","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790682155406,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ecf91b780010w4aHH4s7GOs5S","messageID":"msg_0ecf91547001kCYsqcAvMEQEpu","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"Baseline deaths: all random monsters, plus one starvation (seed 13). So starvation is *mostly* not the baseline's binding constraint — the wizard dies to monsters.\n\nNow, what kills the wizard? Weak monsters (jackal, bat, newt, kitten(!), hobbit, grid bug) and stronger ones (goblin, wolf, ape, soldier ant, rothe, unicorn, white unicorn).\n\n\"killed by a kitten\" — the wizard's own pet killed it?! That's hilarious and suggests real problems (the pet blocks/swaps places: \"You swap places with your kitten\" appeared 34 times!). The kitten is constantly interfering.\n\nHmm, interesting. The pet eats corpses and swaps places. \"You stop. Your kitten is in the way!\"\n\nOK. Let me now think about the strongest lever with the *most* statistical power. \n\nHonest assessment: the death is dominated by a chaotic sequence. Any change gives ~0 mean. To get a reliable gain, I need something that reliably adds a LOT of effective HP or XP.\n\nOptions ranked by expected effect:\n1. **Casting force bolt at range** — re-rolls deaths, ~0 measured.\n2. **Zap the wand of magic missile (8 free charges)** — 8 free 1d6+1 hits ≈ 8 kills. Also re-rolls deaths.\n3. Something that increases XP rate without changing survivability much.\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider the problem from the metric's perspective. Look at the baseline distribution: 8 seeds reach Xp:8, i.e. the wizard sits at XP 8 for a long time. Xp:8 → 0.0745, Xp:9 → 0.117, Xp:10 → 0.179. The distribution is bimodal: most seeds cap at Xp:8, and a few (4, 11) push to 9-10.\n\nTo move the mean meaningfully I need to push many seeds from Xp:8 to Xp:9+, or from Xp:6 to 8+.\n\nThe wizard is at XP 8 by turn ~15000-20000 and then... stalls? Seed 4 got XP 10 at 32346 turns. So after XP 8, XP gain slows dramatically (XP 8→9 needs a lot). The wizard is at max depth 1-5, killing slowly.\n\nWhat if the wizard simply needs to *level the spellcasting skill* / learn better spells? Force bolt at 1 bolt is 1d6+1 — the same as the staff. A *fireball* or a *magic missile* spell would be far better. But learning spells takes time and the wizard only has force bolt + haste self.\n\nOK, let me stop speculating and use the data. The 45-seed A/B will tell me if the spell idea is real. Let me check on it.\n\nMeanwhile, let me think about what else is cheap to test.\n\nActually — here's a thought experiment that suggests something important. Let me compute, for the baseline, the XP-vs-turn curve. From seed 0: xp2@316, xp3@1234, xp4@2943, xp5@5567, xp6@7812, xp7@?, xp8@? (baseline reached Xp:6 max at 13697 — wait, the timeline said (7812, 6), (12834, 5), (12851, 6). So seed 0 maxed at XP 6! But the arena said Xp:6. Yes.)\n\nAnd candidate seed 0: xp2@994, xp3@1679, xp4@2700, xp5@4135, then lost it at 7900. And cand2 (the version that got 20594 turns) reached Xp:8.\n\nSo the candidate DOES accelerate XP substantially: xp5 at 4135 vs 5567 (-26%), and reached xp8 at ~20000 vs the baseline never reaching xp8 on seed 0.\n\nHmm, so on seed 0 the spell was a big win (+0.038). On other seeds it was a loss. Net zero. The variance is the problem, and it's inherent to death-driven scoring.\n\nSo: to convert the spell's XP acceleration into a reliable mean gain, I ALSO need to reduce deaths... but that's a second change.\n\nGiven the constraints (\"one focused change\"), maybe the right call is: keep the spell change (it demonstrably accelerates XP by ~25-30% and cut panics 122→20) and accept the mean is a wash? No — the task's success criterion is the score.\n\nHmm. Let me look at the A/B result when it lands, then decide. Let me check progress.\n\nActually, while waiting, let me consider a *strictly better* variant of the same idea that should reduce the variance and increase the mean:\n\n**Only cast when the target is at range >= 2 AND the wizard has a comfortable energy margin.** Rationale: at range >= 2 the cast is a strict improvement over walking into melee (no risk, free kill). In melee, keep the staff. This makes the change purely *additive*: it adds ranged kills without altering the (working) melee behavior, so the death trajectory changes less → lower variance. And the energy spent is only on genuinely ranged situations.\n\nThat's the design I'd bet on. Let me prepare it as variant B, and test it after the 45-seed A/B of the current variant.\n\nLet me check on the A/B progress.","time":{"start":1790682143609,"end":1790682155381}}}
