{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790682767400,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ed02943f001hfu523TCU0e7k7","messageID":"msg_0ed028e990016APTG05fCs9Kee","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"The baseline mean on 45 seeds is 0.0512, and the per-seed standard deviation is 0.0328, so the standard error of a 15-seed mean is 0.0085. That confirms: differences below ~0.017 (2 SE) are not detectable with 15 seeds.\n\nWith 45 seeds, SE = 0.0328/√45 = 0.0049. Still large relative to the effects I'm seeing (~0.008). But for a *paired* comparison the SE of the mean difference is much smaller because the per-seed deltas are correlated... though here they aren't really correlated (chaotic). The paired-delta SE will be ≈ sqrt(2)·0.0328/√45 ≈ 0.0069 in the worst case (uncorrelated), and much lower if correlated.\n\nSo even 45 seeds gives SE ~0.007. My observed candidate deltas were ~-0.001 to -0.008. So I need a change with a mean effect > ~0.015 to be confident.\n\nThis is a hard measurement problem. Let me use the 45-seed runs to pick the best variant, and use a *low-variance secondary metric* to guide the design: mean turns survived, and mean XP. Those have lower variance than the milestone score and are causally upstream.\n\nLet me look at the baseline 45's turns distribution and the milestone distribution, then compare with the candidate when it finishes.\n\nLet me fix the milestone print.","time":{"start":1790682764351,"end":1790682767387}}}
