{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790683574882,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ed0eaf52001a3n1BYCsBcHdM1","messageID":"msg_0ed0ea6f0001uSfma7aUqIcvXv","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"The 45-seed A/B confirms: **the spell change is a wash** (+0.0011, se 0.0068, t=0.16, 21F/21B). The apparent seed-0-14 result (F6/B9) was noise.\n\nSo the current variant is NOT a win. I need a stronger change or a different one.\n\nKey data:\n- mean turns 15114 → 14578 (-536, slightly worse)\n- The spell accelerates XP but the death re-roll cancels it.\n\nLet me now think hard about the strongest systematic lever.\n\nFacts:\n- Mean turns 15114, median 14486. Every seed ends in death (end_status 1).\n- Score is XP-driven; most seeds land at Xp:8 (13/45), Xp:7 (6), Xp:6 (9).\n- The wizard dies to random monsters at every level.\n- It has a pet kitten that interferes (swaps places 34 times on seed 0, and seed 6 died \"killed by a kitten\"!).\n\n**Big idea: the pet.** \"You swap places with your kitten\" — the pet is constantly disrupting the wizard, causing it to waste turns and take hits from monsters. And it ate corpses (starvation fuel). Seed 6's death was \"killed by a kitten\" — presumably a *hostile* kitten? No, the message would be \"killed by a kitten\" for a hostile cat. Hmm, wiz-hum-cha-mal starts with a kitten pet; if it attacks... Actually the pet can't kill you. So \"killed by a kitten\" = a hostile cat. OK.\n\nAnother observation: the wizard spends 1382/13697 turns SEARCHING and issues `# terrain` 169 times (each costs turns? no, it's a look command). Searching for traps on dlvl 1 is probably a waste — dlvl 1 has few traps. 1382 search actions out of 15013 steps = 9%. If the wizard spent those turns killing, it might gain ~19 more kills (at 72 turns/kill) = maybe +1 XP level on a long run! That's potentially a big win on long runs.\n\nHmm, that's a \"focused change\": stop searching on dlvl 1. But is it seed-agnostic and principled? Yes: NetHack dlvl 1 (the starter level, \"Dungeon level 1\") has essentially no traps worth searching for at low XP; searching is a trap-hunting strategy that pays off on dlvl 2+.\n\nBut careful — the search behavior might be part of `explore`/`escape` strategies. Let me look at where SEARCH comes from. If it's `search_strategy`, disabling it on dlvl 1 is a small, focused change.\n\nHmm, but I need to be careful about what \"one focused change\" means. The task said: make one focused change to raise the score, with a hypothesis comment, no seed-specific tuning, preserve the contract, and improve most seeds.\n\nLet me reconsider. Let me look for the *highest-leverage* thing. Let me quantify the opportunity:\n- 45 seeds, mean 15114 turns, XP8 average progress.\n- If the wizard killed 20% faster, it would get maybe +1 XP level on the long runs → +0.04 on those seeds. On the seeds at Xp:8+ the next milestone is Xp:9 = 0.117 (+0.043). That's a big deal: seeds at Xp:8 (13 of 45) would jump to 0.117 if they reached Xp 9. \n\nSo the payoff structure: the biggest jumps come from pushing Xp:8 → Xp:9. 13/45 seeds are at Xp:8. And Xp:9 requires... a lot of XP (probably ~2000 XP? At XP 8→9 you need 2^(9-1)*... hmm, NetHack XP for level n is 10*n*(n-1)/2... For wiz, XP thresholds: L1:0, L2:20, L3:50, L4:100, L5:200, L6:350, L7:600, L8:900, L9:1400, L10:2000, L11:2800, L12:3700... So XP 8→9 needs 500 more XP, and each kill gives ~10-30 XP (a newt is ~1 XP, a kobold ~5). 500 XP at ~15 XP/kill = 33 more kills = 33*72 = 2400 turns. So on a 25000-turn run that's very achievable — seed 4 did reach Xp:10 at 32346 turns.\n\nSo a 10-20% improvement in kill rate could push many Xp:8 seeds to Xp:9 (+0.043 each). With 13/45 seeds at Xp:8, a 40% conversion would give 5 seeds × 0.043/45 = +0.0048. Hmm, modest.\n\nAlternatively pushing Xp:7→Xp:8 and Xp:6→Xp:7 for the 15+6 = 21 seeds below 8: +0.0377 each.\n\nHonestly, the biggest single jump available: seeds currently at Xp:6 (9 seeds) → Xp:8 = +0.0376, and Xp:7 (6 seeds) → Xp:8 = +0.0238.\n\nA 25% faster kill rate is what the spell achieved (XP5 at 4135 vs 5567 = 26% faster) — and that produced a wash. Hmm, that suggests the kill rate isn't the binding constraint; *survival* is.\n\nLet me reconsider: in cand2 vs baseline on seed 0, the candidate reached xp5 at 4135 (vs 5567) — 26% faster. But it died at 21789 vs 13697. So the extra XP was wasted on a shorter life.\n\nTherefore: **survival is the binding constraint**, not XP rate. To raise the score, I need the wizard to live longer.\n\nHow does the wizard die? Random monster attacks. To live longer:\n1. More HP (level up = more HP; also Constitution).\n2. Better AC (armor!). **Does the wizard wear armor?!** A wiz-hum-cha-mal starts naked except a cloak. If the bot finds a robe/coat and doesn't wear it, that's a huge AC loss. Let me check whether the bot equips armor.\n3. Avoid getting into fights at all (flee more).\n4. Regenerate between fights (rest when hurt) — HP regen is a big deal for a 12-50 HP character.\n5. Keep the AC-lowering cloak etc.\n\nLet me check the AC values. In the snapshots I recorded 'ac'. Let me look at the AC over time in seed 0.\n\nIf AC stays at 8-10 (naked) for 13000 turns, equipping armor would be a massive systematic improvement. The bot's `wielding_ranged_weapon`, armor handling... let me grep for ARMOR_CLASS / equip armor in the codebase.\n\nThis is a very promising lead. Let me check.","time":{"start":1790683557714,"end":1790683574877}}}
