{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790683730691,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ed10feea001LSBlg93qPj4dWg","messageID":"msg_0ed10f98c0017AzByYeQROc3L9","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"`search_neighbors_for_traps(offset)` — with `offset=1` (from `trap_search_offset=1`... actually the call site passes `trap_search_offset=1`). The logic: for each neighbor square the wizard stands on, compute how many searches to do. The key part: `if level.was_on[y, x] or level.objects[y, x] in G.TRAPS: continue`.\n\n`MG_OBJPILE` — the \"specials\" flag for an object pile. The logic gives `offset + 4 - c` searches near object piles — i.e., **it searches around every object pile to find hidden items**! That's the main purpose: searching around item piles to reveal buried gems/armor/weapons.\n\nSo the 14% search time is \"search around object piles to find buried loot\". That IS valuable (finding armor, weapons, gold) — it found the iron skull cap, runed dagger, etc. So disabling it is a gamble, not a clear win.\n\nHmm. OK, so searching is a deliberate feature. Not the lever.\n\nLet me reconsider. Let me re-focus.\n\nI have ~limited time and each 45-seed eval is ~13 min. Let me decide what to test.\n\nGiven:\n- The score is XP-milestone driven, and the bot farms dlvl 1 to XP 8.\n- 28/45 seeds stall at XP 6-8, mostly by dying.\n- Fainting (starvation) 44 times on seed 0.\n\nThe **starvation** angle is the one recurring, clearly pathological behavior. 44 faints in 13697 turns means the wizard's nutrition is chronically at zero. A wizard that isn't starving would: (1) never faint (each faint = 1 HP = near-certain death), and (2) have more effective HP.\n\nWhy is it starving? A wiz-hum-cha-mal starts with a small stomach. It eats lichen corpses (nutrition ~ 1-3?). It picked up a food ration and a fortune cookie. It has `eat_from_inventory` and `eat_corpses_from_ground`.\n\nThe strategy order in `global_strategy`:\n1. `eat_corpses_from_ground(only_below_me=True)` if hunger_state >= NOT_HUNGRY\n2. `eat_corpses_from_ground()` .every(5) if hunger_state >= NOT_HUNGRY\n3. `eat_from_inventory()` .every(5)\nthen `follow_guard`, `fight2`, ...\n\nSo eating IS preempted before fighting and before exploration. Good. But `Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY` gating means it won't eat corpses until it's actually not-hungry (which is quite hungry).\n\nHmm, so the bot does eat. 44 faints means it *can't* find enough food. Is there food on dlvl 1? Corpses of killed monsters. If the wizard kills 190 monsters, there are 190 corpses to eat... but \"You see no objects here\" 38 times, and eating a corpse costs 1 turn + the corpse is often gone (the kitten eats them! \"The kitten picks up a lichen corpse\", \"The kitten drops an uncursed spellbook\").\n\n**THE KITTEN EATS THE FOOD.** On seed 13, \"its pet eats corpses and hunger\". The kitten picks up corpses, and the wizard can't eat them. The kitten is a net negative: it eats the wizard's food supply, blocks movement (\"You swap places with your kitten\" 34 times, \"Your kitten is in the way!\"), and is a liability.\n\n**Focused change: stop the pet from eating the wizard's food (or dismiss the pet).** In NetHack you can `A.Command.QUIT`... no. You can set the pet to \"stay\" with the `P` command (`:p` — the \"P\" extended command to give pet orders: \"P<enter>\" = stay, \"P<follow>\" = follow, \"Pd\" = drop, \"Pk\" = kill, \"P@>\" = dig, \"Pt\" = take, etc.). Also `A.Command.LEASH`/`PET`?\n\nThat's a very focused, principled change: **the pet eats the wizard's food and blocks its movement**. Setting the pet to \"stay\" (or making it not eat corpses) would restore the food supply.\n\nBut hold on — is that \"one focused change\"? Yes, and it's a well-motivated hypothesis: \"the starting kitten eats the corpse pile the wizard needs for calories, and blocks the wizard's escape route; 44 faints from starvation in 13697 turns.\"\n\nHmm, but the risk: the kitten also kills monsters (\"The kitten bites the lichen\", \"The kitten bites the kobold zombie. The kobold zombie is destroyed!\"), which gives... hmm, does the pet's kill give the wizard XP? No, only your own kills give XP. So the kitten contributes nothing but harm? It does help in combat by hitting things.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, does pet kills give XP? In NetHack, XP is for the player's kills. A pet's kill gives no XP (it gives \"experience\" only... actually no, pet kills give no XP).\n\nSo the kitten: no XP contribution, eats food, blocks movement, can be killed. Setting it to \"stay\" or dismissing it is a strong candidate.\n\nBut is there a simpler, even more reliable change? Let me quantify the food economy. Let me count the \"eat\" actions: 392 EAT actions in 13697 turns. And 44 faints. Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider: what's the actual nutrition budget? A wiz's max nutrition is 900 (small stomach), and it loses ~1 nutrition per 2-3 turns of normal activity. 13697 turns × 0.4 = 5500 nutrition needed. Lichen corpses give ~5. A newt corpse ~20. Killing 190 monsters would give ~3800 nutrition total IF it ate them all. So it's roughly break-even — and it can't get them all (the kitten eats some, and it only eats when \"not hungry\").\n\n**So the food supply is genuinely marginal.** The fix that gives the most headroom: eat *more often* (not just when NOT_HUNGRY), so the wizard never drops to 0. Eating a corpse costs 1 turn and is cheap. If it ate at \"hungry\" rather than \"not hungry\", it would never faint.\n\nThat's a one-line, well-motivated change: lower the hunger threshold for eating corpses from `NOT_HUNGRY` to `HUNGRY` (or `WEAK`).\n\nHmm, but eating corpses has a cost: 1 turn each, and it can make the wizard sick from corpses, and it *reduces* carrying capacity constraints... Also, in NetHack, eating a corpse is how you get food; the wizard's `total_nutrition` matters.\n\nLet me combine the insight: the single highest-leverage, most defensible change is about **hunger management**, since 44 faints in one run is pathological and each faint is a near-death.\n\nBut which specific form? Options:\n(a) Eat corpses at HUNGRY instead of NOT_HUNGRY.\n(b) Tell the pet to stop eating corpses (pet stays).\n\nBoth target the same root cause. (a) is simpler and uses existing machinery. \n\nLet me estimate the effect. If fainting goes from 44 → 0, the wizard stops dying to 1-HP situations. That should meaningfully extend life.\n\nHmm, but actually let me check WHY it faints: is it \"You faint from lack of food\" (starvation) or \"You faint\" from damage? The message was \"You faint from lack of food. You regain consciousness.\" — so it's starvation.\n\nBut `events: {'faint': 44}` — the `faint` event. Let me check where that's logged: probably in `agent.update` when 'You faint' appears.\n\nAnd note: the faints are *concentrated at the end* of the run (from the last_messages, turn 7964+). Looking at c0.json's last messages: faints from turn 7964 to 8042 — a tight cluster at death. And the baseline seed 0: 44 faints. Let me check when they happened — if they're all at the end (when the wizard is XP 5-6 and can barely find food), then it's a late-game death spiral.\n\nLet me check the distribution of faints over time in the seed-0 run. The last_messages list should have timestamps. Let me look at all faints with turn numbers. I need the baseline run's messages. Let me re-run the baseline diag and dump messages with turn numbers, filtering for faint.\n\nThat's another 2 minutes but worth it. Let me also check \"You swap places with your kitten\" counts.\n\nActually, my diag only records `last_messages` (the final N). Let me instead use msg_hist from stdout (counts only) — not enough for timing.\n\nLet me just add a faints-with-turn log to the diag. Quick edit.\n\nActually — the msg_hist from the seed-0 baseline run would tell me how many faints there are, and I have \"faint: 44\". To get the timing I need per-turn. Let me modify diag to record a timeline of key events (faint, eat, regain consciousness, swap places, etc.) with turn numbers. One run = 2 min. Let me do it, and while it runs, think.\n\nLet me write it.","time":{"start":1790683709162,"end":1790683730684}}}
