{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790683775056,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ed11cb2c001YzdgYOOAU44wc3","messageID":"msg_0ed11c369001FZ2nMH3w3wFDTY","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"Very revealing:\n- Faints: 5 scattered early (1461, 2576, 4580, 5507, 9414, 10915, 11861, 12831), then a **dense cluster from turn 12831 to 13669** — 36 faints in the last 900 turns. The wizard entered a death spiral of starvation at turn ~12800 and died at 13697.\n- **0 \"eat\" events recorded** — the `You eat ...` regex never matched! The wizard's eating messages are like \"This lichen corpse tastes okay\" / \"You finish eating the lichen corpse\" — which don't match `You eat (.*?)\\.`. So my regex missed. Not conclusive.\n- pet_swap 108 times! \"You swap places with your kitten\" — 108 times in 13697 turns. The pet constantly displaces the wizard.\n- pet_item 56 times: the kitten picks up/drops items 56 times.\n- pet_block 20 times: \"Your kitten is in the way!\" 20 times — the wizard *wasted turns* because its pet blocked it.\n\nSo: the kitten swaps 108 times, blocks 20, and handles 56 items (eating corpses/spellbooks!).\n\nThe death spiral at turn 12800+ is the proximate cause of death on this seed.\n\n**Hypothesis: the starting kitten is a net negative.** It blocks movement (wasted turns, and possibly stepping into monsters), eats the corpse food supply, and contributes no XP.\n\nThe most surgical fix: **dismiss the pet** (or set it to stay). In NetHack, `A.Command.QUIT`... no. To dismiss a pet you just... there's no dismiss in 3.6 (there is `F` = force, and pets leave when you hit them with F, or you can `P` → there's a \"pet\" menu in 3.7 but in 3.6 it's the `:` extended command `p` for pet commands? Hmm.\n\nIn NetHack 3.6, you can give pet commands via the `P` extended command: `P` then a command:\n- `P<Enter>` = stay\n- `P<letter>` = follow?\n- `Pd` = drop\n- `Pk` = kill\n- `Pq` = quit/dismiss? Hmm, actually in 3.6 `P` followed by nothing shows pet status.\n- To dismiss: `P` then `d`... no.\n\nActually, in NetHack 3.6, `A.Command.KICK` with the `F` (force) modifier on a pet makes it attack you... and a sufficiently injured pet flees.\n\nThe simplest pet-order command available: **`P` then `s` (stay)** — hmm. Let me recall the actual 3.6 pet command menu:\n```\n$ P  (pet commands)\nYour pet: the kitten\n>  (command list)\n```\nIn 3.6, pressing `P` shows the pet status and then you enter a command: `d` (drop), `k` (kill), `@` (dig), `t` (take), `s` (swap places?), `f` (follow), `a` (autopickup), `p` (put), `c` (carry), `w` (wear), `q` (quit/dismiss!), `F` (force), `u` (unleash), `l` (leash), `r` (rename), `:` (more info)...\n\nYes! In NetHack, `P` + `q` = \"quit\" → **dismisses the pet**. That's a real command: `Pq` gives \"You abandon your pet\" or similar.\n\nSo a focused change: **dismiss the starting kitten** — `A.Command.EXTCMD` → 'P' → 'q'.\n\nHmm, but is dismissing the pet clearly good? The kitten:\n1. Bites monsters (helps clear paths, but gives no XP).\n2. Swaps places 108 times (wastes the wizard's turn! \"You swap places with your kitten\" — this is the *wizard* moving and swapping with the pet; if the wizard is fleeing or moving to a monster, this WASTES the turn doing nothing useful).\n3. Blocks 20 times (\"Your kitten is in the way!\" — a wasted turn).\n4. Eats food (56 item events, many corpses).\n\nSo the kitten wastes turns, eats food, and gives no XP. Removing it should strictly help an XP-farming wizard.\n\nRisk: the kitten kills monsters, and... no XP. And it might body-block a corridor trap. Minor.\n\n**This is a strong, focused, seed-agnostic hypothesis.** And it targets the measured pathologies directly (108 swaps + 20 blocks + food theft + the starvation spiral).\n\nLet me verify `P` + `q` works in this NLE build. Let me probe it.\n\nAlso let me reconsider: rather than dismissing, `P` + `f` (follow) would stop the swaps? No, the swaps happen because the pet follows you. `P` + `s` (stay) makes it stay put → no swaps, no blocking, but it also stops eating corpses. That's a gentler change than dismissing.\n\nHmm, \"stay\" would leave the pet behind; it can't be attacked then. But when you change levels it follows you (a staying pet doesn't follow you down stairs). \n\nWhich is better? Dismissing is cleaner (no pet at all, no resurrection weirdness). But `P`+`q` in 3.6 — let me check that it's supported. Let me probe.\n\nLet me write a probe: P, then check the message/popup, then try 'q'.\n\nAlso there's a risk: in some versions `P` needs the pet to be able to hear you (\"Your pet doesn't understand that\" if the pet is asleep/deaf). The kitten is probably awake.\n\nLet me probe it.","time":{"start":1790683761452,"end":1790683775051}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1790683779515,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_function_p8rxtncj2k79_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"\"\"\"Probe the pet command (P) and try to dismiss the starting kitten.\"\"\"\nimport contextlib, os, sys, tempfile, threading, time, warnings\nfrom pathlib import Path\ncache = Path(tempfile.gettempdir()) / \"nethack_arena_submission_cache\"\ncache.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\nos.environ.setdefault(\"XDG_CACHE_HOME\", str(cache / \"xdg\"))\nos.environ.setdefault(\"NUMBA_CACHE_DIR\", str(cache / \"numba\"))\nwarnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\", category=RuntimeWarning)\nsys.path.insert(0, \"/workspace\")\nfrom nethackers.arena.environment import NLEEnvironment\nfrom nethackers.arena.seeds import trajectory_spec\nfrom nle.nethack import ACTIONS as _ACTIONS\nfrom nle.nethack import actions as Ac\nfrom autoascend import agent as A\n\n\nclass DirectEnv:\n    def __init__(self, env):\n        self.env, self.agent, self.t0, self.budget = env, None, time.time(), 1e18\n\n    def step(self, action):\n        res = self.env.step(_ACTIONS.index(action))\n        if time.time() - self.t0 > self.budget:\n            raise RuntimeError(\"budget\")\n        return res[0], res[1], res[2], {}\n\n    def debug_tiles(self, *a, **k):\n        return contextlib.nullcontext()\n\n    def debug_log(self, *a, **k):\n        return contextlib.nullcontext()\n\n\ndef run():\n    env = NLEEnvironment(1_000_000, 10_000, \"wiz-hum-cha-mal\")\n    env.reset(trajectory_spec(\"public\", \"local\", 0))\n    d = DirectEnv(env)\n    ag = A.Agent(d, panic_on_errors=False)\n    d.agent = ag\n    with ag.atom_operation():\n        ag.step(Ac.Command.ESC)\n        ag.step(Ac.Command.ESC)\n        ag.character.parse()\n    for _ in range(30):\n        ag.direction('.')\n    print(\"=== EXTCMD P ===\")\n    with ag.atom_operation():\n        ag.step(Ac.Command.EXTCMD)\n        ag.type_text('P')\n    print(\"msg:\", repr(ag.message))\n    print(\"popup:\", list(ag.popup))\n    for cmd in ('q', 'd', 's', 'f'):\n        with ag.atom_operation():\n            ag.step(Ac.Command.EXTCMD)\n            ag.type_text('P')\n            ag.type_text(cmd)\n        print(f\"--- P{cmd}: {ag.message!r}\")\n    for _ in range(5):\n        ag.direction('.')\n    print(\"tty rows containing 'pet' or 'kitten':\")\n    tty = ''.join(chr(c) for c in ag.last_observation['tty'][::-1].reshape(-1))\n    for w in tty.split():\n        if 'kitten' in w or 'pet' in w.lower():\n            print(\"   \", w)\n    env.close()\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.setrecursionlimit(100000)\n    threading.stack_size(256 * 1024 * 1024)\n    t = threading.Thread(target=run)\n    t.start()\n    t.join()\n","filePath":"/home/agent/nhwork/probe6.py"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/home/agent/nhwork/probe6.py","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"home/agent/nhwork/probe6.py","time":{"start":1790683779505,"end":1790683779513}},"id":"prt_0ed12017d001Jd09Ms5J9rgaWC","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","messageID":"msg_0ed11c369001FZ2nMH3w3wFDTY"}}
