{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790683802750,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ed123bf6001u7NIzbdiK3SalQ","messageID":"msg_0ed1235640011Xe0TizikjaZ8Q","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"No PET command in NLE's action set. But `A.Command.CALL` exists — in NetHack 3.6, `C` is \"call\" (monster pet) and also \"chat\". Hmm. In 3.6, the pet command is `C`? Let me think: In NetHack 3.6, `C` = \"Call\" which summons a monster or **toggles the pet-command menu**? Actually in 3.6, `C` is the \"call\" command for monsters; pet commands are under... \n\nIn NetHack 3.6:\n- `C` = Call (summon a monster) — but if the monster is your pet, `C` gives pet commands? Actually yes! In 3.6, `C` on your pet gives the pet command menu: \"Your pet: the kitten\" with options.\n\nHmm, in 3.6.0: `C` (call) — \"if you have a pet, C gives you the pet command menu\"? I recall `C` = call, and for pets you use... Actually in 3.6 the pet command is indeed `C`! From the 3.6 guide: \"C — Call a monster... If the named monster is your pet, you get a list of commands for it.\"\n\nHmm, alternatively `A.Command.CHAT` = 'C' (chat) in 3.6 — chat and call are both bound to C depending on the monster type. And `A.Command.CALL` in NLE might be a different key.\n\nLet me just probe: press `A.Command.CALL` and see the message.\n\nActually, an important realization: the `# pray` output tells me that `EXTCMD` + 'P' opened the extended command menu showing \"# p\" prefix. So the extended command list is alphabetical and 'P' matches \"pray\", \"pay\", \"poison\", \"polymorph\", \"pray\"...\n\nFor pet: in 3.6 the extended command list includes \"pet\" ? Hmm, no.\n\nLet me try: EXTCMD then 'p' to see the p-menu, and CALL directly. Let me probe both.\n\nActually hold on. Let me reconsider whether the pet is even worth chasing. 108 swaps + 20 blocks + food theft. But how much do those actually cost the score? The pet's food theft contributes to the starvation spiral which caused 36 faints and death at 13697.\n\nHmm, but I can't easily dismiss the pet if NLE doesn't expose the command. Let me probe CALL and the 'p' extended command.\n\nAlternatively: a much simpler and more robust fix targeting the same root cause — **eat more / earlier**. If the wizard eats at HUNGRY rather than NOT_HUNGRY, it banks nutrition and never hits 0.\n\nLet me look at the numbers: the death spiral starts at turn ~12800. Before that, faints at 1461, 2576, 4580, 5507, 9414, 10915, 11861, 12831 — so it faints ~8 times early, then spirals. The wizard reached XP 6 and just ran out of food.\n\nSo the wizard's total food income is marginal. Eating *earlier/more* doesn't create more food; it just spreads the same total. If the income is less than the expenditure, it'll still hit zero eventually. UNLESS eating corpses that the pet ate / that it skipped is the fix — i.e., the food IS there but the wizard doesn't collect/eat it.\n\nKey: \"eat_corpses_from_ground\" only eats corpses ON THE GROUND. If the kitten picks up the corpse first, the wizard can't. 56 pet_item events — some are corpses. So dismissing the pet recovers maybe 10-30% of the food.\n\nHmm, this is getting speculative. Let me reconsider the *entire* approach given my remaining budget.\n\n**Time/compute check**: I've used a lot. Each 45-seed eval is ~13 min. I should pick ONE strong change and validate it.\n\nLet me reconsider the strongest evidence I have:\n1. The spell change: measured +0.0011 ± 0.0068 over 45 seeds → neutral. Not a win, but not a loss. It also *accelerates XP by 25%* (mechanistically verified) and cuts panics 122→20. Its problem: survival.\n2. AC 9 → armor is a supply issue.\n3. The pet: 108 swaps, 20 blocks, food theft.\n4. Starvation death spirals.\n\nThe single change with the best combination of *mechanism* and *reach* is the pet, IF I can dismiss it. It attacks all three pathologies at once: wasted turns (swaps/blocks), food theft (starvation spiral → faints → death), and it contributes zero XP.\n\nLet me probe how to dismiss the pet. In NLE, `A.Command.CALL` might be it. Let me test.\n\nLet me also consider: instead of dismissing, `P` commands could be sent via `A.Command.CHAT` ('C'). Let me probe both CALL and CHAT and see what messages appear.","time":{"start":1790683790326,"end":1790683802744}}}
