{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790683828018,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ed128282001E6eBFVoNCF2Rrc","messageID":"msg_0ed127d48001n4TViA38Srt29y","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"No pet command available in NLE. `CALL` is drink (the quaff/f command), `CHAT` is talk-to. The extended command menu has no \"pet\" entry (it echoed \"# ppet\" as unknown).\n\nSo I **cannot dismiss the pet** in this NLE build. The pet-management change is off the table.\n\nOK. Let me refocus with a clear head on what I CAN do and what has the best measured mechanism.\n\nConstraints/learnings:\n- Can't dismiss the pet.\n- The spell change is neutral (measured over 45 seeds).\n- Score is XP-milestone driven; survival is the binding constraint.\n- Noise SE on 45 seeds ≈ 0.007.\n\nLet me reconsider the *strongest* remaining lever: **the wizard dies to monsters while at low HP after repeated damage.** The `emergency_strategy` handles emergencies. What about *resting to heal*? A wiz at XP 1-4 has 12-38 max HP. NetHack's natural HP regeneration heals 1 HP per ~2 turns at low HP (fast regen at HP < 1/3 max, slower above). So a wizard can heal ~0.5-1 HP/turn while resting.\n\nKey question: does the bot rest to heal? Let me look at `exploration_strategy(None).until(lambda: hitpoints >= 0.8*max_hitpoints)` — there's a `until` on hitpoints which means the exploration strategy runs *until* HP >= 80%... Actually `.until(self.agent, condition)` repeats the strategy until the condition is true. So `exploration_strategy(None).until(hp >= 0.8*max)` keeps exploring while HP < 80% max! That's backwards — it means the bot keeps fighting/exploring while hurt, and only stops exploring once healed (which happens passively).\n\nHmm, actually `until` semantics: `Strategy.until(agent, condition)` — \"repeat the wrapped strategy until condition is true\". So it explores until HP ≥ 80%. So while hurt it explores more. That's... fine, exploration includes resting.\n\nLet me look for a rest/heal strategy. `A.Command.SIT` exists (rest). Is there a rest strategy? Let me grep for SIT/REST.\n\nActually, I recall the exploration module has a \"rest until healed\" behavior. Let me check.\n\nHmm, I'm running low on time. Let me make a decision.\n\n**Decision**: The spell change is my best *measured* candidate: mechanistically it accelerates XP by 25% (verified via XP timelines on 2 seeds) and cuts panics 6x, and the 45-seed paired eval is +0.0011 (not a loss). But it's not a win.\n\nLet me improve it with the one refinement that has a clear mechanism and directly addresses the failure: **only cast at range ≥ 2** — this makes the change purely additive (never displaces melee, where the staff is free) and prevents spending 5 energy on point-blank fights where the wizard is exposed anyway.\n\nHmm, but that reduces the XP acceleration.\n\nAlternatively — and I think this is stronger — **the real problem with the spell change is that it costs turns to regenerate energy.** Energy regen: +1 per 2 turns while resting (NetHack gives energy faster when resting: 1 per turn if fully rested and not moving? Actually energy regen is 1 per 2 turns normally, but a *resting* character regenerates 1 energy per turn). So a 5-energy cast costs 5 turns of resting vs ~10 turns of normal regen.\n\nIf the wizard *rests* (searches/waits) while it has no monsters to fight, energy regenerates at 1/turn. So casting is fine if the wizard rests when it has no target. Does it? It searches 14% of the time (which is resting-ish).\n\nOK, let me just be empirical. Let me run the range≥2 variant over 45 seeds and compare against the 45-seed baseline I already have. That's 13 min. And simultaneously I should consider: is there a variant with a much bigger expected effect?\n\nLet me think about the *biggest* possible lever once more, because a 13-min-per-experiment loop only supports a few tries.\n\n**Insight attempt**: the milestone is `BE_ON_FIRST_LEVEL` = \"be on the first dungeon level\", whose condition to advance is `experience_level >= 8`. The bot farms dlvl 1 until XP 8, then moves to the next milestone. 28/45 seeds never reach XP 8.\n\nWhat if the bot could gain XP *much* faster on dlvl 1? The main XP source is kills. 190 kills → XP 6. So ~32 XP/kill average. To get to XP 9 (600 more XP) needs ~19 more kills = 1400 turns. So the wizard at 13697 turns killed 190 monsters but is XP 6 (needs ~350 XP for XP 6). Hmm, 350 XP / 190 kills = 1.8 XP per kill. So the kills are *trash* (newts, lichens, kobolds). To gain XP faster it must kill *stronger* monsters — which means descending!\n\nBut descending is dangerous for a 12-HP wizard. Hmm, the bot's milestone design deliberately farms dlvl 1 to XP 8 first.\n\nWhat if it went deeper earlier? The baseline's max_depth shows several seeds reach depth 4-5 (seeds 2, 8, 9, 11 and one Dlvl:5). Those got XP 8-10.\n\nSo: **descending earlier** would raise the XP rate. But that's risky and the milestone logic is deliberate.\n\nI think the honest conclusion: the highest-confidence improvement available within \"one focused change\" is the spell usage, and I should tune it to maximize the measured effect. Let me do ONE more 45-seed run with the refined variant (range≥2 + no wasted attempts) and pick the best of the two.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider one thing about the spell change that might be a *bug* rather than a tuning issue: the spell kills monsters at range, which means **the wizard takes fewer hits** (good) but also **the pet and the wizard's melee never get exercised**... no.\n\nHmm, here's a potential *real* problem: `cast_priority` returns priority 20 for ANY monster in the ray, including dangerous ones, and it's checked BEFORE melee. When the wizard is adjacent to a monster, it now casts instead of meleeing. Force bolt at level 1 = 1 bolt = 1d6+1. An unskilled quarterstaff = 1d6. So the spell is barely better, costs 5 energy (= 5-10 turns of regen), and while casting you're doing nothing else. Meanwhile melee costs 0 energy.\n\nSo at range ≥ 2 casting is clearly good (a free kill without closing distance). At range 1 it's marginal-to-bad. That supports the range≥2 refinement.\n\nLet me implement: `cast_priority` returns None if the target is adjacent. Then melee handles point-blank, and the spell handles ranged. This is cleaner, more defensible, and less disruptive.\n\nLet me implement and run the 45-seed eval.\n\nActually, one more important consideration: with range≥2 only, the wizard will cast less often, saving energy → more turns for exploration → probably better survival. Good.\n\nLet me also double check: should the priority be 20 (above melee) or should I place it below ranged (dagger throw at 11)? A dagger throw from range 2-3 does ~1d4+0. Force bolt does 1d6+1 and can't miss. So the spell should beat the dagger throw. Keep 20 (it only matters when adjacent, and adjacent is now excluded). Let me keep 20.\n\nLet me make the change and launch the 45-seed run.","time":{"start":1790683808386,"end":1790683828010}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1790683831496,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_function_3r7xnmn2mrz1_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/autoascend/combat/fight_heur.py","newString":"def cast_priority(agent, dy, dx, monsters):\n    \"\"\" Priority for throwing a known attack spell down the ray (dy, dx).\n\n    Mirrors ranged_priority's geometry exactly -- same walk, same blockers -- so a\n    spell is only ever offered at a monster we can actually hit.\n\n    Only for monsters at range. Point blank, the spell is strictly worse than the\n    quarterstaff it would replace: it deals about the same damage for 5 energy, and\n    energy is the scarcest thing a wizard owns. At range it is the other way round,\n    because a ranged attack needs no energy at all.\n    \"\"\"\n    spell = agent.character.best_attack_spell()\n    if spell is None:\n        return None\n\n    y, x = agent.blstats.y, agent.blstats.x\n    while True:\n        y += dy\n        x += dx\n        if not inside(agent, y, x):\n            return None\n\n        if agent.glyphs[y, x] in G.PETS or not agent.current_level().walkable[y, x]:\n            return None\n\n        if agent.glyphs[y, x] in G.MONS:\n            monster = [m for m in monsters if m[1] == y and m[2] == x]\n            if not monster:\n                # there is a monster that shouldn't be attacked\n                return None\n            assert len(monster) == 1\n            if adjacent((y, x), (agent.blstats.y, agent.blstats.x)):\n                return None\n            return 20, spell, y, x, monster[0]\n","oldString":"def cast_priority(agent, dy, dx, monsters):\n    \"\"\" Priority for throwing a known attack spell down the ray (dy, dx).\n\n    Mirrors ranged_priority's geometry exactly -- same walk, same blockers -- so a\n    spell is only ever offered at a monster we can actually hit.\n    \"\"\"\n    spell = agent.character.best_attack_spell()\n    if spell is None:\n        return None\n\n    y, x = agent.blstats.y, agent.blstats.x\n    while True:\n        y += dy\n        x += dx\n        if not inside(agent, y, x):\n            return None\n\n        if agent.glyphs[y, x] in G.PETS or not agent.current_level().walkable[y, x]:\n            return None\n\n        if agent.glyphs[y, x] in G.MONS:\n            monster = [m for m in monsters if m[1] == y and m[2] == x]\n            if not monster:\n                # there is a monster that shouldn't be attacked\n                return None\n            assert len(monster) == 1\n            return 20, spell, y, x, monster[0]\n"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /workspace/autoascend/combat/fight_heur.py\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/autoascend/combat/fight_heur.py\n+++ /workspace/autoascend/combat/fight_heur.py\n@@ -101,8 +101,13 @@\n     \"\"\" Priority for throwing a known attack spell down the ray (dy, dx).\n \n     Mirrors ranged_priority's geometry exactly -- same walk, same blockers -- so a\n     spell is only ever offered at a monster we can actually hit.\n+\n+    Only for monsters at range. Point blank, the spell is strictly worse than the\n+    quarterstaff it would replace: it deals about the same damage for 5 energy, and\n+    energy is the scarcest thing a wizard owns. At range it is the other way round,\n+    because a ranged attack needs no energy at all.\n     \"\"\"\n     spell = agent.character.best_attack_spell()\n     if spell is None:\n         return None\n@@ -122,8 +127,10 @@\n             if not monster:\n                 # there is a monster that shouldn't be attacked\n                 return None\n             assert len(monster) == 1\n+            if adjacent((y, x), (agent.blstats.y, agent.blstats.x)):\n+                return None\n             return 20, spell, y, x, monster[0]\n \n \n def get_next_states(agent, wand, y, x, dy, dx):\n","filediff":{"file":"/workspace/autoascend/combat/fight_heur.py","patch":"Index: /workspace/autoascend/combat/fight_heur.py\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/autoascend/combat/fight_heur.py\n+++ /workspace/autoascend/combat/fight_heur.py\n@@ -101,8 +101,13 @@\n     \"\"\" Priority for throwing a known attack spell down the ray (dy, dx).\n \n     Mirrors ranged_priority's geometry exactly -- same walk, same blockers -- so a\n     spell is only ever offered at a monster we can actually hit.\n+\n+    Only for monsters at range. Point blank, the spell is strictly worse than the\n+    quarterstaff it would replace: it deals about the same damage for 5 energy, and\n+    energy is the scarcest thing a wizard owns. At range it is the other way round,\n+    because a ranged attack needs no energy at all.\n     \"\"\"\n     spell = agent.character.best_attack_spell()\n     if spell is None:\n         return None\n@@ -122,8 +127,10 @@\n             if not monster:\n                 # there is a monster that shouldn't be attacked\n                 return None\n             assert len(monster) == 1\n+            if adjacent((y, x), (agent.blstats.y, agent.blstats.x)):\n+                return None\n             return 20, spell, y, x, monster[0]\n \n \n def get_next_states(agent, wand, y, x, dy, dx):\n","additions":7,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"workspace/autoascend/combat/fight_heur.py","time":{"start":1790683831484,"end":1790683831493}},"id":"prt_0ed12d0230018cCQ4S5mufFpkf","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","messageID":"msg_0ed127d48001n4TViA38Srt29y"}}
