{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790683852585,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ed130a53001c9ka1KeRth1bXU","messageID":"msg_0ed13001c001ZngSiI7j1WErgS","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"Not relevant. Let me look at the exploration loop for a \"rest when hurt\" behavior — i.e., does the wizard wait/search until healed? NetHack's `search` action is effectively a rest (it advances a turn and regens HP/energy). The bot searches 14% of the time, so it does rest passively. And `explore_stairs`/`patrol` etc.\n\nOK. Let me accept that I don't have time to find a dramatically better lever and focus on getting a solid, defensible, measured result.\n\nLet me use the waiting time productively: let me quantify the *variance* question. With 45 seeds, the paired SE was 0.0068. The two spell variants both need >0.014 to be credible. If the true effect is ~0.005, I can't demonstrate it. \n\nSo maybe I should consider: is there a change with an effect size of 0.03+? That would require moving the *mean* by ~1.5 milestones.\n\nLet me think about what could do that... \n\n**Reconsider: the death spiral from starvation is the single most common proximate cause.** 44 faints on seed 0, and the run died in a food death spiral. Baseline seed 13 died of starvation outright. If I could eliminate starvation deaths entirely, the affected seeds (seed 13 directly; seed 0's death spiral) would live substantially longer.\n\nHow to eliminate starvation? The wizard must eat more. Its food income: corpses on the ground + food rations. The bot eats corpses only when `hunger_state >= NOT_HUNGRY` (i.e., when it's already getting hungry). And `eat_corpses_from_ground(only_below_me=True)` eats only a corpse under the wizard.\n\n**A focused change: eat corpses as soon as the wizard is merely hungry, and eat any corpse on the ground rather than only below.** Currently:\n- `eat_corpses_from_ground(only_below_me=True)` when NOT_HUNGRY\n- `eat_corpses_from_ground()` every 5 turns when NOT_HUNGRY\n- `eat_from_inventory()` every 5\n\nIf the wizard has 900 max nutrition and each corpse gives ~20-40, it needs ~25 corpses to be full. It killed 190 monsters → 190 corpses = ~5000 nutrition available, vs ~5500 needed. Marginal! But if it ate the *first* corpse it saw instead of the nearest/only-beneath, it would bank nutrition and never hit zero.\n\nHmm, \"eat_corpses_from_ground\" — how far does it look? Let me check. If it only eats corpses it walks over, and it walks over few, it misses most.\n\nThe most direct fix: **change `NOT_HUNGRY` to `HUNGRY`** in those conditions, so the wizard starts eating earlier and banks nutrition. That's a 1-token change with a clear mechanism: \"the wizard enters a starvation death spiral because it only starts eating once it is already NOT_HUNGRY, and it fainted 44 times; eating from HUNGRY keeps the buffer topped up.\"\n\nHmm, but if the total food income is marginally below expenditure, banking just delays the spiral.\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider: does NetHack's nutrition actually get consumed that fast? For a level-1 wizard, nutrition drains based on `morehungry`/movement. 13697 turns with ~5000 nutrition lost. And it had 44 faints. If the wizard had eaten better it wouldn't have fainted at all. With 190 kills, corpses everywhere, the food is there.\n\nThe `eat_corpses_from_ground` gating on `NOT_HUNGRY` plus `.every(5)` means the wizard only looks for corpses every 5 turns when not hungry. If the wizard is at 100/900 nutrition, it doesn't bother. Then it wanders off, burns to 0, faints, then eats in a panic.\n\n**This is a real, fixable inefficiency.** And the fix is exactly \"eat earlier\". Let me look at `eat_from_inventory` and `eat_corpses_from_ground` to make a precise change.\n\nLet me look at the code.","time":{"start":1790683843155,"end":1790683852561}}}
