{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1790683854993,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ed1338830010SMDmy1tIyMtXF","messageID":"msg_0ed1330c6001EK4fOBo27lgacV","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790683865636,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ed13388b0018J5Uk43nk4g4wv","messageID":"msg_0ed1330c6001EK4fOBo27lgacV","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"`eat_from_inventory` already triggers at `hunger_state < Hunger.HUNGRY` → yields False, so it eats at HUNGRY or hungrier. Good.\n\nThe corpse-from-ground strategies are gated at `>= NOT_HUNGRY` in global_logic, and `eat_corpses_from_ground()` (all over the level) runs only `.every(5)`.\n\nNote the `.every(5)` — that means \"run at most once every 5 turns\"? Or \"wait 5 turns between\"? Let me check `Strategy.every`. If it means the strategy only fires every 5th turn, then corpse-eating from the whole level is only considered every 5 turns. Combined with the `NOT_HUNGRY` gate, the wizard is slow to find food.\n\nA focused change: gate those corpse strategies on `>= Hunger.HUNGRY` instead of `>= Hunger.NOT_HUNGRY`. Rationale: bank nutrition early; the wizard fainted 44 times because it waits until it is already quite hungry to look for food, and it enters an unrecoverable spiral.\n\nHmm, but `eat_from_inventory` at HUNGRY already fires — the wizard has food items (food ration, fortune cookie, corpses in inventory? it eats lichen/lizard corpses from inventory). So the wizard IS eating at HUNGRY from inventory. Hmm! So why does it starve?\n\nBecause the wizard doesn't *collect* corpses. Its inventory has: daggers, darts, potions... a food ration, a fortune cookie, a blindfold. It doesn't pick up corpses (probably deliberately, to avoid the \"corpse in inventory\" bloat).\n\nSo: the wizard has almost no food in inventory, and relies on eating corpses off the ground, which only happens at NOT_HUNGRY. When it drops to hungry-but-not-not-hungry, it explores; when it hits NOT_HUNGRY it eats a corpse. So nutrition hovers low and eventually a spiral (as at turn 12800: 36 faints in 900 turns).\n\nHmm, during a spiral, `NOT_HUNGRY` is satisfied so it SHOULD eat corpses. Why didn't it? \"You see no objects here\" 38 times. Maybe there were no corpses left — the level was picked clean, or it was in a food-free area.\n\nOK, this is a plausible but unproven fix. It's a 2-token change (`NOT_HUNGRY` → `HUNGRY`) in global_logic.\n\nGiven limited compute, which do I test: the refined spell (running now) or the hunger change?\n\nThe refined spell is running. Let me wait for it. If it's a clear improvement, keep it. If neutral, I'll test the hunger change next, since it's cheap and orthogonal (and I could even combine them... but \"one focused change\" — hmm, the instruction says one focused change. I've already got the spell change implemented. If I add the hunger change, that's two. I should pick ONE and revert the other.)\n\nHmm, the instruction: \"Make one focused change in /workspace/autoascend/ ... raise the score ... with a # hypothesis comment\". I should deliver ONE change. So I need to choose between the spell change and the hunger change (or something else) based on measured results.\n\nLet me prepare the hunger variant now so I can test it immediately after the spell run finishes. Actually, I can't test both in parallel (1 CPU).\n\nLet me check the spell run's progress.\n\nActually — an important thought. Let me reconsider the problem completely differently, because I keep finding that per-seed outcomes are chaotic.\n\n**What if the biggest lever is the milestone itself?** `BE_ON_FIRST_LEVEL` requires XP 8 on dlvl 1. The bot farms dlvl 1 until XP 8. 17/45 seeds never reach XP 8 (they end at XP 2-7). If instead the bot progressed to deeper levels earlier, the XP rate would be much higher (stronger monsters = more XP per kill) — and indeed the seeds that reached depth 4-5 got XP 8-10.\n\nThe condition `experience_level >= 8` on dlvl 1 is a design choice by the bot's author. Changing it to, say, XP 5 would get the wizard deeper sooner... and probably die more. Chaotic again.\n\nOK. Decision time. Let me be pragmatic:\n\nThe spell change is *measured* to be neutral (+0.0011 ± 0.0068). The refined version may be slightly better. Neither is a demonstrated win.\n\nRather than keep gambling on 13-minute experiments, let me consider what gives the best *expected* outcome: the change with the clearest causal story AND non-negative measurement. The spell change has:\n- verified 25% XP acceleration\n- 6x fewer panics\n- measured mean effect ≥ 0\n- a real mechanism (uses an unused class mechanic, never touches melee at range ≥ 2 now)\n\nI'll test the refined version, and if it's non-negative I'll keep it as my one change. Let me also make sure I remove the temporary env switch before finishing.\n\nLet me check the run.","time":{"start":1790683854987,"end":1790683865612}}}
