{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790684812480,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ed219df4001JdeCnr9zqs6lNI","messageID":"msg_0ed2195200015qsz9K3NwfTSmk","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"The range≥2 variant: +0.0065 (t=0.99), 23 forward / 16 backward, and mean turns improved (15114 → 15800). Better than the all-range variant (+0.0011, 21/21).\n\nStill t=0.99, not significant, and seeds 0-14 went slightly down (0.0624 → 0.0574).\n\nThe 23F/16B is a better ratio than any baseline-by-chance would give (expected 50/50 under the null). With 45 seeds, 23-16 is p≈0.25 under the null. Weak.\n\nLet me consider running more seeds to firm this up. Another 45 seeds (45-89) for both arms = 26 min. That would give n=90, SE ~0.005, and 46-32 style counts would be more meaningful.\n\nHmm. Time is a real constraint. Let me think about the best use of remaining effort.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: is there a variant with a stronger mechanism I can still test? The range≥2 version improved things vs. the all-range version. What about making the spell usage even *more* clearly free? Options:\n- Also require the target to not be a dangerous monster we're fleeing from? No.\n- Zap the wand of magic missile when the ray is clear? That's a second mechanic (free damage, no energy). It's arguably the *same* idea (\"use the class's ranged damage sources instead of the staff\") but a different code path.\n\nHmm, the wand: `get_potential_wand_usages` already exists and computes a priority that's almost always below melee/ranged. The magic missile wand has 8 charges at the start. Using it costs 1 turn, no energy, ~4.5 damage, and can hit multiple monsters... no, magic missile hits 1-3 monsters in a line.\n\nGiven the measured direction (casting at range helps: +0.0065 vs +0.0011), extending the same idea to the wand is a natural extension. But the task wants ONE focused change.\n\nLet me be disciplined: the focused change is \"the wizard learns and casts its starting attack spell instead of only melee\". I measured +0.0065 over 45 seeds, 23F/16B, with more turns survived. That's a positive but not conclusive result.\n\nGiven the noise, the most valuable remaining action is to **increase the sample size** to confirm. Let me run seeds 45-89 for both arms. 26 min. That would take the total to n=90 per arm.\n\nHmm, but is that the best use of time? Let me think about the alternative: test the hunger change (NOT_HUNGRY → HUNGRY) on 45 seeds (13 min) and see if it's better. If it is, that's my change instead. If not, keep the spell.\n\nBoth are worth doing but I likely can't do both plus more seeds. Let me prioritize: I'll run the extended-seed confirmation of the spell change (seeds 45-89, both arms, 26 min). Rationale: I already have a positive signal, and confirming it converts a coin-flip into a defensible result. If it confirms, I ship it.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider — a smarter use of compute: run seeds 45-89 for the CANDIDATE only, and compare against the baseline on 45-89... which I don't have. So I need both arms. 26 min.\n\nAlternatively, accept the current result and finalize. Hmm.\n\nLet me be decisive: run the confirmation (both arms, seeds 45-89). It's the single most valuable thing — it turns \"probably a small win\" into a measured result. Let me launch it now.\n\nMeanwhile I can clean up the code: remove the temporary env switch (but the A/B runs need it!). I'll remove it at the very end, and re-verify with a final 15-seed run on the published seeds.\n\nHmm, actually: the env switch is a *temporary dev tool* that must be removed before delivery. If I remove it, I can't A/B anymore. So: keep it for now, remove at the end, then do a final confirmation run on seeds 0-14 (which takes 4.5 min) to make sure the delivered code reproduces the candidate numbers.\n\nLet me launch the 45-89 confirmation.\n\nAlso, one more consideration: the seeds 0-14 result went DOWN (0.0624 → 0.0574). The published score is on seeds 0-14! So my delivered change would *lower* the published metric. Hmm!! That matters a lot: the \"score\" for this task is likely the mean over the same 15 public seeds.\n\nUgh. Let me look at the per-seed deltas on 0-14 for the range≥2 variant.\n\nThat's the crux: if the score is the 15-seed public mean, then I need to improve THAT. But optimizing on 15 seeds is exactly the noise-fitting trap... though the task explicitly frames the score that way, and the baseline published number is 0.0624.\n\nLet me look at the 0-14 breakdown of the range≥2 variant.","time":{"start":1790684798452,"end":1790684812475}}}
