{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790684863585,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ed228e43001v7Q61T7jD7Y9NF","messageID":"msg_0ed22869f001cHR7j6h7EFS4WC","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"Note: I just moved `learn_spells` in global_logic while the ab3 A/B is running! That will affect the *candidate* arm of ab3 (it imports the code at process start... but each `python -m nethackers.arena.run` is a separate process launched sequentially; the baseline arm runs first, then the candidate arm). If the candidate arm already started, my edit won't affect it. If it hasn't started yet, the candidate arm will run with the moved strategy.\n\nThis is a problem for the experiment's validity! Moving `learn_spells` after the eat strategies is a behavioral change (in practice it should be a no-op since at game start there's nothing to eat and the wizard is not hungry — the eat strategies yield False). Let me think: at the start, hunger_state is 0 (not hungry), so `eat_corpses_from_ground().every(5)` requires hunger >= NOT_HUNGRY → False. `eat_from_inventory()` requires hunger_state >= HUNGRY → False. So all three yield False and fall through to `learn_spells`. So the reordering is behaviorally a no-op in practice.\n\nBut it's still a risk for the experiment. Let me check where ab3 is. If the candidate arm already started before my edit, no problem. Let me check the log.\n\nActually, more importantly: the process for the candidate arm starts AFTER the baseline arm completes. The baseline arm takes ~13 min. I started ab3 at about the time... let me check the log to see if the baseline finished.\n\nLet me check.","time":{"start":1790684859971,"end":1790684863559}}}
