{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1790684866103,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ed22a61f0019GqVtwMyLyLCC3","messageID":"msg_0ed229db2001DpKyQt12Iwz3Av","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790684873708,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ed22a62b001N4w6vUqCT7sgf8","messageID":"msg_0ed229db2001DpKyQt12Iwz3Av","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"The baseline arm is still running (only 3 arena lines so far, no output file). The candidate arm will start ~13 min from now and will pick up my edited global_logic.py. Since the reordering is a behavioral no-op (nothing to eat at the start, hunger below all thresholds), the experiment remains valid. But to be safe and rigorous, let me revert the reorder until after the A/B completes, then re-apply it. Hmm — that adds complexity and risk of forgetting.\n\nBetter: let me verify the reorder is truly a no-op by reasoning once more:\n- `learn_spells` triggers only when `known_spells` is empty, which is only at the very start of the game (turn ~1-40).\n- At that point: hunger_state = 0 → `eat_corpses_from_ground(only_below_me=True).condition(hunger >= NOT_HUNGRY)` → NOT_HUNGRY is presumably 1, so 0 >= 1 is False → yields False.\n- `eat_from_inventory` → `if hunger_state < HUNGRY: yield False`. HUNGRY is a large value → yields False.\n- So the three eat strategies all yield False immediately, and the preemption chain falls through to `learn_spells` either way.\n\nThe only difference would be if the eat strategies had side effects before yielding False — they don't (they yield False before acting).\n\nSo the reorder is a strict no-op for the spell-learning behavior, and the A/B stays valid. Good, I'll keep it.\n\nNow let me wait for the A/B and meanwhile double-check a few robustness items:\n\n1. `read_spellbook` uses `item.object.name`. Is `item.object` a property that exists on Item? Let me check — in inventory_items.py there was `item.object`? Let me verify. In the diag I used `it.object.name` and it worked. Good.\n\n2. `item.is_unambiguous()` — exists on Item. Used it, and the code imported OK; but is the method name right? The 45-seed runs exercised `learn_spells` successfully (force bolt was read: 130 \"In what direction?\" casts happened), so yes.\n\n3. What if the wizard picks up an *ambiguous* spellbook (e.g. \"a spellbook\")? `is_unambiguous()` guards that. Good.\n\n4. What if the wizard is not a Wizard? Guarded by `self.character.role != Character.WIZARD`. Other roles are unaffected. Good — important since the codebase is generic.\n\n5. Contract: `make_agent()` unchanged. Let me verify.\n\nLet me check the adapter contract is untouched.\n\n6. One risk: `parse_spellcast_view` is now generic (no longer Healer-only). For a Healer, `should_cast_heal` uses `self.character.spell_fail_chance['healing']` and `known_spells['healing']`. With my rewrite, if the popup doesn't parse as expected, `known_spells` would be empty and `should_cast_heal` might KeyError. Let me check: `should_cast_heal` does `if self.character.spell_fail_chance.get('healing', ...)`. Let me look at it to be sure I haven't broken the healer path — even though this task is only about the wizard, breaking the healer would be bad practice.\n\nLet me check should_cast_heal and who calls parse_spellcast_view.","time":{"start":1790684866092,"end":1790684873682}}}
