{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790686664715,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ed3dd867001H2xAaaYOgXa0Y4","messageID":"msg_0ed3dd2490014rVIj5UhwAuOn4","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"Definitive: over 90 paired seeds, the spell change is **-0.0029 ± 0.0054** (t=-0.54, p=0.59), 39 forward / 40 backward / 11 same, and mean turns dropped 17290 → 15932.\n\nSo the spell change is a **wash, trending slightly negative**. It does NOT raise the score. The seeds-15-44 block was noise; seeds 45-89 went the other way.\n\nI must not ship a change that doesn't raise the score. I need to either:\n(a) Revert everything and report that no focused change survived measurement, or\n(b) Find a different change that actually helps.\n\nLet me think about (b) with the time I have. I have measured: the per-seed outcome is chaotic; SE on 45 seeds is ~0.005-0.007; and 90 seeds gives SE 0.0054. To detect a real win I need an effect > 0.011 at n=90. That's a big ask.\n\nBUT: there's one signal I haven't exploited — **mean turns survived**. base 17290 → cand 15932. The spell change *reduces* survival. That's a real, low-variance regression signal. So the spell hurts survival.\n\nWhy would casting at range hurt survival? A plausible mechanism: **the wizard kills monsters at range instead of letting them come to it, so it never levels up by fighting, and more importantly, ranged kills mean the wizard keeps exploring more (levels are picked clean) — or, critically, the energy cost means it rests more, and resting in a hostile level is dangerous.**\n\nHmm. Whatever. It's not a win.\n\nLet me now consider the remaining plausible change with a strong, low-variance mechanism. Given everything measured, what reliably *increases* turns survived?\n\nFrom the data I have (baseline, 45 seeds): mean turns 15114 (seeds 0-44) / 17290 (seeds 0-89). Everything dies.\n\nThe one systematic pathology I found with a low-variance, mechanistic story: **the starting kitten**. 108 position swaps, 20 blocked turns, and food theft that drives a 44-faint starvation spiral. I can't dismiss it (no NLE command).\n\nSecond: **AC 8-9 the whole game** (armor supply).\n\nThird: **14% of steps spent searching** for buried loot on early dungeon levels.\n\nHmm, the searching one is interesting: it costs 14% of steps and 2050 turns on seed 0. The purpose of `search_neighbors_for_traps(offset=1)` is to dig up loot from object piles. On dlvl 1 the object piles are the starter items. The payoff is real (it found the skull cap and a runed dagger).\n\nBut here's the thing: 2050 searches is ~14% of the run. If instead it spent those turns killing, at 72 turns/kill that's ~28 extra kills ≈ +28 XP... wait, XP per kill is ~1.8, so 28 kills = 50 XP. Hmm, that's ~0.1 of an XP level. Not huge.\n\nHmm wait, let me redo that. Baseline seed 0: 190 kills, reached XL 6. XP for XL 6 (wiz) = 350. So 350/190 = 1.84 XP per kill. Extra 28 kills = 51 XP. Going from 350 to 400 XP doesn't cross the XL 7 threshold (600). So no score change. Not the lever.\n\nOK so what DOES change the score? Only crossing a milestone (XL 7, 8, 9...). XP thresholds: XL7=600, XL8=900, XL9=1400, XL10=2000. So from XL 6 (350) to XL 7 (600) needs 250 more XP = 136 more kills = 9800 turns. That's why seeds stall.\n\nTo cross a milestone you need ~100+ extra kills. At 72 turns/kill that's 7000+ extra turns. So the ONLY ways to move the score are: (a) dramatically faster kills, or (b) live much longer.\n\nSince the bot is at ~17290 mean turns and dies, and it needs ~25000-30000 turns to reach XL 8-9... the seeds that DO reach XL 8-10 are the long-lived ones.\n\nSo: **survival is everything**. A change that increases mean turns by 30% would push many seeds over a milestone.\n\nWhat increases survival the most?\n- Not getting into fights at all (avoidance).\n- Healing (resting).\n- AC (supply-limited).\n- HP (leveling = survival).\n\nLet me look at the emergency/flee logic — maybe the bot fights to the death too often. `ONLY_RANGED_SLOW_MONSTERS` handling suggests it tries to kite. Let me check how often the bot flees vs fights.\n\nHmm. I've spent a lot of effort. Let me make a judgment call about how to close this out responsibly.\n\n**Honest position**: I have a 90-seed paired experiment showing the spell change is neutral-to-slightly-negative. I cannot ship it as a score improvement. The right thing is to revert it and report that the focused change I implemented did not survive measurement, with the evidence.\n\nBut the task asked me to make a change that raises the score. Let me try ONE more thing with a strong mechanism and low variance, then stop.\n\nGiven the analysis, the highest-leverage remaining idea with a *direct* effect on survival: **let the wizard heal**. Let me check: does the bot ever deliberately rest until healed? Line 600 in global_logic: `exploration_strategy(None).until(agent, hitpoints >= 0.8*max_hitpoints)` — this runs the exploration strategy *until* HP ≥ 80%. Inside exploration, does it rest/wait? `explore1` has a `wait_action` and searches. Searching/waiting regenerates HP. So it passively heals while exploring.\n\nHmm, so it does heal.\n\nOK — let me reconsider. What about the *opposite* of casting: the score is hurt by the wizard dying. What if the single most effective survival change is to **flee more**? Let me look at whether the bot flees at all. Let me check `emergency_strategy` and the flee logic thresholds.\n\nActually, let me do something smarter and cheaper: instead of guessing, let me measure where the *baseline* dies. I have cause_of_death for 90 seeds. Let me tabulate the causes — if most deaths are to specific monster classes, that tells me what to avoid.\n\nLet me do that quickly. It's free (data already collected).","time":{"start":1790686648423,"end":1790686664709}}}
