{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790686689084,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"id":"prt_0ed3e27aa001mBqQOvrHvou5lS","messageID":"msg_0ed3e2047001fGSjih0yr9vmGC","sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","type":"reasoning","text":"The causes of death are almost perfectly uniform across ~60 different monsters — no systematic weakness to exploit. 6/90 starvation, 84/90 killed by something. That's the signature of pure chaos: the wizard dies to whatever it happens to meet last.\n\nThis is strong evidence that **no single behavior tweak will produce a reliable, large score change** — the outcome is dominated by which monster happens to land the killing blow. Any change re-rolls that dice, and the mean stays put. My 90-seed experiment confirms it empirically (39F/40B).\n\nGiven that, the responsible engineering decision is:\n\n**Revert the change.** It does not raise the score, and shipping a change that measures as neutral-to-negative (while adding ~150 lines across 6 files) is worse than not shipping it. The task asked for a change that raises the score; I should report honestly that the focused change I built and measured did not survive a 90-seed paired test, and explain why (with the evidence: uniform cause-of-death distribution → chaotic outcome; SE 0.0054 means anything under ~0.011 is unmeasurable at this sample size).\n\nHmm. But wait. Let me reconsider whether reverting is really the best call, or whether I'm giving up too early.\n\nThe instructions said to make one focused change that raises the score. I attempted it, measured carefully, and it doesn't. Options:\n1. Revert and report the negative result.\n2. Keep searching for a change with a bigger effect.\n\nThe one thing I *haven't* tried that has a genuinely different, large mechanism: the milestone/strategy structure. The bot deliberately farms dlvl 1 until XL 8 (`Milestone.BE_ON_FIRST_LEVEL`). This is a *design choice* that caps the XP rate: dlvl 1 trash gives ~1.8 XP/kill and 72 turns/kill. The seeds that scored best (Xp:10, Xp:9) descended to depth 4-5.\n\nA change like \"go deeper earlier\" has a much bigger potential mechanism (XP per kill rises 5-10x on dlvl 3-5). But it's also the riskiest (a 12-46 HP wizard descending dies fast). Given the uniform death distribution, it's another dice re-roll... but the *expected* XP rate would genuinely increase, which is a real mechanism, not just a re-roll. Hmm, and the milestone progression in global_logic has a `condition` that advances the milestone; the next milestone after BE_ON_FIRST_LEVEL is FIND_SOKOBAN, which requires reaching Sokoban. So the bot would descend to find Sokoban once it hits XL 8.\n\nTesting \"lower the XL 8 threshold to e.g. XL 4 and descend\" would be a legitimate focused change with a big mechanism. But it changes the bot's whole plan and I have ~1 more 45-seed experiment's worth of realistic time (13 min each).\n\nGiven the measured reality, let me be honest with the user rather than gamble another 26 minutes and end up with nothing. Actually — no. Let me reconsider. I have a real opportunity: I have 90 baseline seeds measured. If I test ONE more candidate on the same 90 seeds, I get a directly comparable number (26 min).\n\nWhich candidate has the best chance of a >0.011 effect? Honest ranking:\n- Descend earlier: big XP-rate mechanism, but big death risk. Genuinely uncertain.\n- Eat earlier (NOT_HUNGRY→HUNGRY): small mechanism (6/90 starvation deaths + reduces the faint spirals that lead to other deaths). Effect likely < 0.01.\n- Zapping the wand of magic missile (8 free charges at start, priority currently below everything): adds free damage, similar in spirit to casting but energy-free. Casting measured -0.003; the wand is strictly better (no energy). Could plausibly be positive. But it's a second variant of the same failed idea.\n\nHonestly, the highest-expected-value single test is \"descend earlier\", because it changes the *plan* rather than the *combat*, and the XP-per-kill difference is orders of magnitude (dlvl 1 newt ≈ 1 XP; dlvl 4 rothe ≈ 25 XP).\n\nBut it's also 26 min and I'd need to be careful.\n\nLet me reconsider the time budget. I've been going a long time. The user asked \"Continue if you have next steps, or stop and ask for clarification if you are unsure how to proceed.\"\n\nI think the right move: revert the measured-neutral change (so I leave the workspace in a clean, defensible state) and report the finding + ask whether to invest in the higher-risk \"descend earlier\" experiment. That respects the user's agency and doesn't burn 26 more minutes on a gamble without confirmation.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider once more. Reverting means the deliverable is \"no change\". The task explicitly wants a change that raises the score. But shipping a change that *lowers* the score (or is neutral with 90-seed evidence) would be worse — it's the kind of thing that looks like progress but isn't.\n\nLet me do this: revert the spell change, and clearly present (a) the evidence, (b) the one high-upside idea I haven't tested, and ask before spending the compute. That's the honest, high-integrity close.\n\nHmm, but actually — wait. Let me reconsider ONE more time whether there's a cheap, high-confidence, *positive* change I can justify without a 26-minute gamble.\n\nLooking at the data once more: 6/90 died of *starvation*. That's 6.7% of seeds, and starvation deaths are worth ~0.08 each (they die early, at low XP). Fixing hunger perfectly would be worth maybe 6 × 0.06 / 90 = +0.004. Small but positive, and starvation is the *one* cause with a clear mechanism and a controllable fix. Combined with the faint-spiral evidence (44 faints on seed 0 leading to death).\n\nExpected +0.004 at SE 0.0054. Undetectable. Not worth it alone.\n\nDecision: revert, report, ask. Let me revert cleanly.\n\nReverting: I need to undo all 6 files. Let me do it by reversing each edit precisely. Since I have all the original text in my edits, let me revert each one.\n\nFiles to revert:\n1. `autoascend/character.py`: remove `import os`, remove ATTACK_SPELL_DAMAGE, remove the 3 dict inits, restore the original parse_spellcast_view, remove spell_energy_cost/can_cast/best_attack_spell.\n2. `autoascend/agent.py`: remove `import os`, restore the original cast(), restore direction() (with the inline dict), remove `_direction_action`, remove the 'cast' branch, remove _read_spellbooks init, remove read_spellbook/learn_spells + the comment.\n3. `autoascend/combat/fight_heur.py`: remove cast_priority, the cast block, restore the melee/ranged tuple, remove the Character import.\n4. `autoascend/global_logic.py`: remove the learn_spells preempt.\n5. `autoascend/stats_logger.py`: remove the 2 counters.\n6. `autoascend/combat/utils.py`: remove the 'cast' action_str branch.\n\nThat's a lot of manual reverting with risk of error. \n\n**Much safer alternative**: keep the change but disable it? No — that's shipping dead code.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, let me reconsider: is reverting definitely right? Let me weigh:\n- 90-seed evidence: -0.0029 ± 0.0054. Statistically indistinguishable from 0, but the point estimate is negative and mean turns dropped 8%. There's no positive evidence to ship it.\n- The 15 published seeds: -0.0050.\n\nSo no, it shouldn't be shipped as an improvement.\n\nLet me revert. To do it safely and verifiably, I'll reverse each edit and then confirm the reverted files behave identically to the original by re-running the 15 published seeds and checking I get exactly the published baseline 0.0624 with the exact per-seed numbers. That's a strong verification that I've restored the original code exactly (I have the exact baseline table).\n\nThat's a good plan: revert + verify by reproducing the published baseline exactly. The verification run is 4.5 min.\n\nLet me do the reverts.","time":{"start":1790686668714,"end":1790686689080}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1790686695603,"sessionID":"ses_f132db44effejOC7YW0uFAg7SL","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_function_i3d5odt6bacw_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/autoascend/character.py","newString":"    def parse_spellcast_view(self):\n        self.known_spells = dict()\n        self.spell_fail_chance = dict()\n\n        # TODO: parse for other spellcaster classes\n        if self.role not in (self.HEALER,):\n            return\n\n        with self.agent.atom_operation():\n            self.agent.step(A.Command.CAST)\n            if not self.agent.popup:\n                self.known_spells[self.agent.message] = None\n                return\n            if self.agent.popup[0] not in ('Choose which spell to cast') or \\\n                    not self.agent.popup[1].startswith('Name'):\n                raise ValueError(f'Invalid cast popup text format: {self.agent.popup}')\n            for line in self.agent.popup[2:]:\n                matches = re.findall(r'^([a-zA-Z]) - *' +\n                                     r'(' + '|'.join(ALL_SPELL_NAMES) + ') *' +\n                                     r'([0-9]*) *' +\n                                     r'(' + '|'.join(ALL_SPELL_CATEGORIES) + ') *' +\n                                     r'([0-9]*)\\% *' +\n                                     r'([0-9]*\\%|\\(gone\\))', line)\n                assert len(matches) == 1, (matches, line)\n                letter, spell_name, level, category, fail, retention = matches[0]\n                assert len(letter) == 1, letter\n                self.known_spells[spell_name] = letter\n                self.spell_fail_chance[spell_name] = int(fail) / 100\n        self.agent.step(A.Command.ESC)\n","oldString":"    def parse_spellcast_view(self):\n        self.known_spells = dict()\n        self.spell_fail_chance = dict()\n        self.spell_levels = dict()\n\n        with self.agent.atom_operation():\n            self.agent.step(A.Command.CAST)\n            popup = list(self.agent.popup)\n\n        if not popup:\n            return\n        if popup[0] != 'Choose which spell to cast' or not popup[1].startswith('Name'):\n            return\n\n        for line in popup[2:]:\n            matches = re.findall(r'^([a-zA-Z]) - *' +\n                                 r'(' + '|'.join(ALL_SPELL_NAMES) + ') *' +\n                                 r'([0-9]*) *' +\n                                 r'(' + '|'.join(ALL_SPELL_CATEGORIES) + ') *' +\n                                 r'([0-9]*)\\% *' +\n                                 r'([0-9]*\\%|\\(gone\\))', line)\n            if len(matches) != 1:\n                continue\n            letter, spell_name, level, category, fail, retention = matches[0]\n            assert len(letter) == 1, letter\n            self.known_spells[spell_name] = letter\n            self.spell_fail_chance[spell_name] = int(fail) / 100\n            self.spell_levels[spell_name] = int(level)\n\n        if self.known_spells:\n            self.agent.step(A.Command.ESC)\n\n    def spell_energy_cost(self, spell_name):\n        \"\"\" Energy the game will charge for casting this spell.\n\n        Measured in-game rather than guessed: one force bolt takes a wiz from 6\n        energy to 1, and 6 is the whole pool a starting wizard owns. Guessing low\n        is expensive -- the game refuses the cast, no turn is spent, and the fight\n        loop re-offers the same impossible action. Spells cost 2 energy per level\n        above the first, so the rest is extrapolated from that one measurement.\n        \"\"\"\n        level = self.spell_levels.get(spell_name, 1)\n        return 5 + 2 * (level - 1)\n\n    def can_cast(self, spell_name):\n        if spell_name not in self.known_spells:\n            return False\n        return self.agent.blstats.energy > self.spell_energy_cost(spell_name)\n\n    def best_attack_spell(self):\n        \"\"\" Name of the best known spell to throw at a monster, or None.\n\n        Reliable beats strong: an unreliable spell just announces your position and\n        wastes energy, and this character's energy bar is its scarcest resource.\n        \"\"\"\n        if os.environ.get('AUTOASCEND_SPELLS', '1') == '0':\n            return None\n        best = None\n        best_key = None\n        for name, fail in self.spell_fail_chance.items():\n            if name not in ATTACK_SPELL_DAMAGE or not self.can_cast(name):\n                continue\n            # A rejected cast burns no turn, so re-offering it immediately would spin\n            # the fight loop on the same impossible action until it trips the\n            # turn-inactivity guard and ends the episode. Energy trickles back at\n            # roughly one point every other turn, so wait long enough to matter.\n            if self.agent._last_turn - self.agent.last_cast_fail_turn[name] < 15:\n                continue\n            key = (fail, -ATTACK_SPELL_DAMAGE.get(name, 0.0))\n            if best_key is None or key < best_key:\n                best, best_key = name, key\n        return best\n"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /workspace/autoascend/character.py\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/autoascend/character.py\n+++ /workspace/autoascend/character.py\n@@ -342,78 +342,35 @@\n \n def parse_spellcast_view(self):\n     self.known_spells = dict()\n     self.spell_fail_chance = dict()\n-    self.spell_levels = dict()\n \n+    # TODO: parse for other spellcaster classes\n+    if self.role not in (self.HEALER,):\n+        return\n+\n     with self.agent.atom_operation():\n         self.agent.step(A.Command.CAST)\n-        popup = list(self.agent.popup)\n+        if not self.agent.popup:\n+            self.known_spells[self.agent.message] = None\n+            return\n+        if self.agent.popup[0] not in ('Choose which spell to cast') or \\\n+                not self.age